var e=JSON.parse('[[301,"The prefers-reduced-motion media query lets you detect whether the user has switched on the system setting for reduced motion. It is used to disable or replace animations, in order to avoid discomfort for people with vestibular disorders. This improves the accessibility of web applications.","What is prefers-reduced-motion?\\nThe `prefers-reduced-motion` media query is a CSS feature that checks the user\'s system setting for reduced animation. It returns one of two values: `no-preference` (the default) or `reduce` (the user has asked for motion to be minimized). This query is part of the CSS Media Queries Level 5 specification and is aimed at improving the accessibility of web content.\\nWhy is this needed?\\nMany people experience discomfort, dizziness or nausea when watching intense animations, especially those with vestibular disorders or migraines. By using `prefers-reduced-motion`, developers can disable or replace animations, making a site more comfortable for such users. It also conforms to the WCAG (Web Content Accessibility Guidelines) recommendations.\\nHow do you use it?\\nThe query is applied in CSS or JavaScript. In CSS you can override animations for users who prefer reduced motion. An example:\\n\\nIn JavaScript you can check the setting through `window.matchMedia`:\\n\\nWhere should you apply it?\\nThis media query is useful on any site with animations: parallax effects, rotating elements, flashing banners, transitions between pages. It is especially important for sites with a lot of movement — portfolios or landing pages, for example.\\nConclusion: use `prefers-reduced-motion` to create an inclusive interface that respects the needs of users sensitive to motion. It increases accessibility and improves the user experience.","/* The ordinary animation */\\n.element {\\n  animation: spin 2s infinite;\\n}\\n\\n/* We disable the animation for users with the reduce setting */\\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\\n  .element {\\n    animation: none;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst motionQuery = window.matchMedia(\'(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)\');\\nif (motionQuery.matches) {\\n  // We disable the animations\\n}"],[302,"Prettier is a tool for formatting code automatically that ensures a single style throughout a project. It is configured through a configuration file — .prettierrc, for example — where you can set parameters such as the line width and the use of quotes and semicolons. Prettier integrates with code editors and can be run automatically when a file is saved or through pre-commit hooks.","What is Prettier and why is it needed?\\nPrettier is an opinionated code formatter that brings code to a single style automatically, putting an end to arguments about formatting within a team. It supports many languages: JavaScript, TypeScript, CSS, HTML, JSON, Markdown and others. Its main purpose is to spare developers from formatting by hand and to make code readable and predictable.\\nHow do you configure Prettier in a project?\\nPrettier is installed through npm or yarn:\\n\\nCreate a `.prettierrc` configuration file in the project\'s root. An example:\\n\\nYou can also add a `.prettierignore` file in order to exclude particular files or folders from formatting — `node_modules` or `dist`, for example.\\nIntegration with the editor and automation\\nFor automatic formatting on save in VS Code, install the Prettier extension and configure it in `settings.json`:\\n\\nTo run it in CI/CD or in pre-commit hooks, use `pretty-quick` or `lint-staged`. An example with husky and lint-staged:\\n\\nConclusion\\nPrettier is worth applying in any project where the consistency of the code and the automation of routine tasks matter. It is especially useful in teams, since it puts an end to discussions about formatting and speeds code review up.","npm install --save-dev prettier\\n\\n{\\n  \\"semi\\": true,\\n  \\"singleQuote\\": true,\\n  \\"tabWidth\\": 2,\\n  \\"trailingComma\\": \\"es5\\"\\n}\\n\\n{\\n  \\"editor.formatOnSave\\": true,\\n  \\"editor.defaultFormatter\\": \\"esbenp.prettier-vscode\\"\\n}\\n\\n// package.json\\n{\\n  \\"husky\\": {\\n    \\"hooks\\": {\\n      \\"pre-commit\\": \\"lint-staged\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\"lint-staged\\": {\\n    \\"*.{js,jsx,ts,tsx,css,md}\\": [\\"prettier --write\\"]\\n  }\\n}"],[303,"Inline styles have a higher priority than ordinary CSS classes, because of the rules of CSS specificity. An inline style\'s specificity is counted as the highest among the simple selectors, so it overrides the properties set through a class. The exception is the use of !important, which can beat an inline style if it is applied in a class.","What is CSS specificity?\\nSpecificity is the mechanism that determines which styles will be applied to an element when several rules conflict. It is calculated from the types of selector: inline styles have the highest weight, then come ID selectors, then classes and attributes, and finally tags and pseudo-elements.\\nThe priority of inline styles\\nInline styles, set through the `style` attribute in the HTML, have a specificity of 1000 (notionally), which is higher than a class (10). This means that any property stated inline will override the same property from a class, unless the class uses `!important`.\\nAn example\\n\\nThe exception: !important\\nIf you add `!important` in the class, it will override the inline style, since `!important` has the highest priority of all the rules, inline ones included.\\n\\nConclusion\\nInline styles are convenient for quick overrides, but abusing them makes code harder to maintain. It is better to use classes and to avoid `!important`, in order to keep the cascade predictable.","<div class=\\"box\\" style=\\"color: red;\\">Text</div>\\n\\n.box {\\n  color: blue;\\n}\\n// The result: the text will be red, since the inline style has priority\\n\\n.box {\\n  color: blue !important;\\n}\\n// Now the text will be blue, despite the inline style"],[304,"Inline styles have high specificity, but they can be overridden with the !important rule in CSS. You can also change the style attribute through JavaScript. Another way is to use the CSS property all: revert, which resets inline styles.","What inline styles are and why they are hard to override\\nInline styles are set directly in an HTML element\'s style attribute. They have very high specificity (1000 points), so ordinary CSS rules cannot override them without special techniques.\\nThe main ways of overriding them\\n\\n- !important — added to a CSS property in order to raise its priority above inline styles. Example:\\n\\nthis will override `style=\\"color: red\\"`.\\n- JavaScript — you can change or remove the style attribute directly:\\n\\n- CSS all: revert — it resets every inline style to its inherited or initial value:\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nIn this example the colour will become blue because of !important, while the font size will stay 20px, since an ordinary rule cannot override the inline one.\\nConclusion\\nUse !important only as a last resort, when inline styles have to be overridden with certainty — in component libraries, for example. For flexible control over styles it is better to avoid inline attributes and to use CSS classes.","div { color: blue !important; }\\n\\nelement.style.color = \'green\'; // or element.removeAttribute(\'style\');\\n\\ndiv { all: revert; }\\n\\n<div style=\\"color: red; font-size: 20px;\\">Text</div>\\n\\n<style>\\n  div {\\n    color: blue !important; /* will override the red */\\n    font-size: 16px; /* will not work, since the inline one is higher */\\n  }\\n</style>"],[305,"To get rid of !important you have to raise the selector\'s specificity, rearrange the order in which the styles are attached, or use more nested selectors. You can also apply the BEM methodology to isolate styles, or CSS modules. It is important to review your CSS architecture and to avoid conflicts through the proper organization of your code.","Understanding the problem with !important\\nThe !important directive in CSS breaks the natural cascade of styles, making a rule take priority regardless of specificity. This often leads to difficulties in maintaining and overriding styles. The main goal is to eliminate such dependencies and restore the cascade\'s predictability.\\nWays of eliminating !important\\n\\n- Raising the selector\'s specificity: use more specific selectors — add a class to the parent element or use an ID, for example. Instead of `.button { color: red !important; }`, write `.container .button { color: red; }`.\\n- Changing the order in which the styles are attached: if two rules have the same specificity, the last one wins. Move the rule you want lower in the file, or attach it later.\\n- Using the BEM methodology: BEM (Block Element Modifier) creates unique class names, which rules out conflicts. For example: `.block__element--modifier { color: red; }`.\\n- CSS modules or CSS-in-JS: these approaches generate unique class names automatically, isolating the styles and removing the need for !important.\\nA practical example\\nSuppose there is a button with !important:\\n\\nThe solution is to remove !important and raise the specificity:\\n\\nOr to use BEM:\\n\\nConclusion\\nEliminating !important improves the maintainability and predictability of your CSS. Apply raised specificity, naming methodologies or style-isolation tools for clean, scalable code.",".btn { color: blue !important; }\\n.special .btn { color: red; } /* will not work */\\n\\n.btn { color: blue; }\\n.special .btn { color: red; } /* now it works */\\n\\n.btn { color: blue; }\\n.btn--special { color: red; }"],[306,"Using innerHTML to insert user input can lead to XSS attacks. An attacker can inject a malicious script that will run in the victim\'s browser. This lets them steal data, redirect the user or change the page\'s content. To insert text safely, use textContent or create elements through DOM methods.","The security problems with innerHTML\\nThe innerHTML method lets you insert HTML code into an element, but if the code being inserted contains user input, that opens the way to XSS attacks. XSS (Cross-Site Scripting) is a vulnerability in which an attacker injects a malicious script into a web page, which is then executed in other users\' browsers.\\nHow it works\\nWhen you use innerHTML, the browser parses the string as HTML and processes every tag in it, including `<script>` and tags with event-handler attributes such as `<img onerror=\\"...\\">`. Even though a `<script>` inserted through innerHTML is not executed by modern browsers, an attribute like `onerror` or `onload` on an `<img>` or an `<svg>` will run, which is enough to steal cookies, read the DOM or send the user\'s data to someone else\'s server.\\nThe safe alternatives\\n\\n- textContent — it inserts a string as plain text, so any markup in it is displayed rather than interpreted.\\n- Creating elements through DOM methods (document.createElement, appendChild) and filling them through textContent or by setting attributes individually.\\n- Sanitizing the HTML with a proven library (DOMPurify, for example), if you genuinely need to insert markup.\\n- A Content Security Policy, as an additional layer that restricts the sources of scripts.\\n\\nConclusion: never pass user input into innerHTML without sanitizing it. Use textContent for text, DOM methods for structure, and a sanitizing library for the rare cases where markup really does have to be inserted.",null],[307,"Specificity in CSS is the way of determining the priority of CSS rules when several conflicting styles apply to the same element. It is calculated from the number of selectors of each type in the rule:\\n\\n- Inline styles (style=\\"color: red;\\", for example) have the highest priority.\\n\\n- ID selectors (#id) are more specific than classes (.class) or tags (div).\\n\\n- The universal selector (*) and the cascade have the lowest priority.\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\n`#id { color: blue; } /* more specific than */\\n.class { color: green; }`","Specificity is a numerical value that CSS uses to determine the priority of rules when they conflict in their application to the same element.\\nHow specificity is calculated:\\nEvery rule has a \\"weight\\" that depends on the types of selector:\\nInline styles: the highest priority, a specificity of `1000`.\\n\\nID selectors: one ID adds `100` to the specificity.\\n\\nClasses, attributes and pseudo-classes: each adds `10`.\\n\\nTag selectors and pseudo-elements: each adds `1`.\\n\\nThe universal selector (`*`) and the cascade have a specificity of `0`.\\nAn example calculation:\\n\\nIf all three rules apply to the same element `<div id=\\"id\\" class=\\"class\\">`, the final colour will be blue, since the ID selector is the most specific.\\nThe effect of the cascade and specificity:\\n\\n- If two rules have the same specificity, the one stated last in the CSS file wins.\\n\\n- `!important` can override any rule, but it should be used with care.\\n\\nThe full example:\\nHTML:\\n\\nCSS:\\n\\nThe result: the text will become blue, since the `#main` style has the highest specificity.\\nPractical use:\\nSpecificity helps developers understand why one style overrides another. This matters especially when working on large projects, where a great many CSS rules may overlap. To avoid confusion, a clear structure of selectors is recommended, along with avoiding excessively complex combinations.","<div style=\\"color: red;\\">Text</div>\\n\\n#main { color: blue; }\\n\\n.highlight { color: green; }\\n[type=\\"text\\"] { color: gray; }\\n\\np { color: black; }\\n\\n/* Specificity: 0-0-1 (a tag only) */\\ndiv { color: black; } \\n\\n/* Specificity: 0-0-10 (a class only) */\\n.class { color: green; } \\n\\n/* Specificity: 0-1-0 (an ID selector) */\\n#id { color: blue; }\\n\\n<div id=\\"main\\" class=\\"highlight\\">Text</div>\\n\\ndiv { color: black; } /* specificity: 0-0-1 */\\n.highlight { color: green; } /* specificity: 0-0-10 */\\n#main { color: blue; } /* specificity: 0-1-0 */"],[308,"The CSS box model is a fundamental concept in CSS describing how the elements on a web page are represented as rectangular boxes consisting of content, margins, borders and padding.","The CSS box model is the basis for understanding how the browser computes the dimensions and placement of the elements on a web page. Every HTML element is treated as a rectangle divided into four areas:\\n\\n- Content — the element\'s actual content: text, images or other nested elements. It is the main part, whose dimensions can be set through `width` and `height`.\\n\\n- Padding — the space between the element\'s content and its border. Padding is added inside the element and can be configured with the properties `padding-top`, `padding-right`, `padding-bottom` and `padding-left`.\\n\\n- Border — the frame around the content and the padding. The border\'s size can be set through `border-width`, and its style and colour through `border-style` and `border-color`.\\n\\n- Margin — the space between the element and its neighbours. The margin controls the distance between elements but does not affect the element\'s own dimensions. It can be configured through the properties `margin-top`, `margin-right`, `margin-bottom` and `margin-left`.\\n\\nWhen computing an element\'s overall dimensions the browser adds together the content\'s width, the `padding`, the `border` and the `margin`. If you have an element with a content width of `200px`, `padding 20px`, `border 5px` and `margin 10px`, for example, the element\'s total width will be `200px` (content) + `20px` (padding on the left) + `20px` (padding on the right) + `5px` (border on the left) + `5px` (border on the right) = `250px`. The margin affects the element\'s placement but is not part of its dimensions.\\nTo control an element\'s dimensions including its `padding` and `border`, the property `box-sizing: border-box;` is often used. It makes the element\'s dimensions already include the `padding` and the `border`, which simplifies work on layouts.\\nAn example:\\n\\nUnderstanding and configuring the box model properly lets you control the positioning of and spacing between the elements on a page easily.","div {\\n  width: 200px;      /* content width */\\n  padding: 20px;     /* padding around content */\\n  border: 5px solid black; /* border around padding */\\n  margin: 10px;      /* margin outside border */\\n}"],[309,"• `display: none` removes an element from the web page entirely, as though it did not exist. It takes up no space in the layout, and the elements after it shift to occupy its space.\\n• `visibility: hidden` hides an element but leaves its space on the page. The element still takes up space in the layout but is not visible to the user.","In CSS the `display` and `visibility` properties both control the visibility of elements, but they do it differently.\\n\\n- `display: none` removes an element from the document\'s flow entirely.\\nThis means it takes up no space, and the other elements on the page will behave as though it did not exist.\\nThe element will not interact with the user and will not be visible.\\nThis property is often used for elements that are hidden dynamically (in modal windows or dropdown menus, for example), where the element disappears entirely and does not affect the page\'s layout.\\n\\n- `visibility: hidden` leaves the element in the document but makes it invisible to the user.\\nThe element keeps its place in the layout — that is, the other elements do not shift or change position.\\nThe element still takes up space and can be shown again with visibility: visible.\\n\\nUse display: none to remove an element completely, when you do not want it to take up space, and visibility: hidden when you need to hide an element but keep its space in the layout.",null],[310,"There are three main ways of attaching CSS to HTML: inline, internal and external. Inline CSS applies styles directly to an element using the `style` attribute. Internal CSS is placed inside a `<style>` tag in the `<head>` of the HTML document and applies to every element on the page. External CSS lives in a separate file with the `.css` extension, which is attached to the HTML through a `<link>` tag in the `<head>`, which lets the same styles be used on several pages.","HTML has three main ways of attaching CSS, each with its own advantages and used in different situations.\\nInline CSS: this way means using the `style` attribute right inside an HTML element. For example:\\n`<h1 style=\\"color: blue;\\">Hello, world!</h1>`\\nInline CSS is convenient for applying styles to individual elements quickly, but it can lead to inefficient code if used too often.\\nInternal CSS: the styles are defined inside a `<style>` tag placed in the document\'s `<head>`. For example:\\n\\nInternal CSS is convenient when you want the styles to apply to one page only. It is not suitable for large projects, however, since it requires duplicating the code on different pages.\\nExternal CSS: this way means creating a separate CSS file, which is attached to the HTML document with a `<link>` tag. For example:\\n\\nExternal CSS is the most preferable method for large projects, since it lets you separate the HTML structure from the styles. This makes maintaining and changing styles easier, because it is enough to make a change in one place and it applies to every page that uses that file.\\nEach of these methods can be useful depending on the context. In most cases external CSS is recommended for organizing code and improving its readability.","<head>\\n  <style>\\n    body {\\n      background-color: lightgray;\\n    }\\n    h1 {\\n      color: green;\\n    }\\n  </style>\\n</head>\\n\\n<head><link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"styles.css\\"></head>"],[311,"Web fonts such as Google Fonts can be attached through a `<link>` element in the `<head>` section of your HTML, or you can use the CSS `@import`. Fonts can slow a page\'s rendering down, however, so it is important to optimize their loading. The main factors affecting performance include the number of font styles, their format and the loading strategy (`font-display: swap`, for example, to avoid blocking the rendering of text).","Integrating web fonts (Google Fonts, for example) is a simple process that lets you use external fonts on your site. It is done through HTML and CSS. One of the most popular ways of attaching web fonts is using Google Fonts through a `<link>` tag in the HTML or the `@import` directive in CSS.\\nAn example of integration through HTML:\\n\\nAn example through CSS:\\n\\nWhen using web fonts it is important to consider performance, since the browser has to load the fonts before it can start displaying the text. Here are several factors that affect loading speed:\\n\\n- The number of fonts and their styles: the more fonts and variations (weights or italics, for example), the more data has to be loaded.\\n\\n- The font format: support for various formats (WOFF, WOFF2, TTF) matters for cross-browser compatibility. WOFF2 is the more compressed, preferable format.\\n\\n- Loading strategies: using the `font-display: swap` property lets the browser display the text with a fallback font first and then replace it with the web font once it has loaded. This prevents empty space on the page while the font is loading.\\n\\nAn example of optimizing with `font-display`:\\n\\nIntegrating and optimizing fonts properly improves the user experience by speeding up page loads and preventing delays in the display of text.","<head>\\n  <link\\n    href=\\"https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Roboto:wght@400;700&display=swap\\"\\n    rel=\\"stylesheet\\"\\n  />\\n</head>\\n\\n@import url(\\"https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Roboto:wght@400;700&display=swap\\");\\n\\n@font-face {\\n  font-family: \\"Roboto\\";\\n  src: url(\\"roboto.woff2\\") format(\\"woff2\\");\\n  font-display: swap;\\n}"],[312,"A dark theme can be implemented with the CSS media query `prefers-color-scheme`, which detects the user\'s preference as to the colour scheme (light or dark). The appropriate styles are applied depending on the browser\'s preference. You can also set up manual theme switching with JavaScript and CSS classes.","Support for a dark theme in web applications lets users switch between the light and dark themes automatically based on their system settings. This is achieved with the CSS media query `prefers-color-scheme`. This media query checks which colour scheme the user\'s system prefers (light or dark) and applies the appropriate styles.\\nAn example of using the media query for a dark theme:\\n\\nThis lets the dark theme be applied automatically when the user\'s device is set to a dark colour scheme.\\nFor manual switching between themes with a button you can use JavaScript. For example, you can keep the theme\'s state in `localStorage` and change classes in the DOM dynamically.\\nAn example with JavaScript for switching the theme by hand:\\n\\nWith this code you can save the user\'s choice and apply it on their next visit to the page.\\nYou can also use meta tags such as `theme-color` to change the browser\'s colour scheme:\\n\\nThese meta tags configure the colour scheme of the browser\'s interface according to the theme. Support for a dark theme makes web applications more adaptive and improves the user experience.","/* The default style (the light theme) */ \\nbody {  \\n  background-color: white;  \\n  color: black; \\n} \\n/* The dark theme */ \\n@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {  \\n  body {    \\n    background-color: black;    \\n    color: white;  \\n  } \\n}\\n\\nconst toggleTheme = () => {  \\n  const theme = document.body.classList.toggle(\'dark\') ? \'dark\' : \'light\';    \\n  localStorage.setItem(\'theme\', theme); \\n}; \\n  \\ndocument.addEventListener(\'DOMContentLoaded\', () => {  \\n  const savedTheme = localStorage.getItem(\'theme\');  \\n  if (savedTheme) {    \\n    document.body.classList.add(savedTheme);  \\n  } \\n});\\n\\n<meta name=\\"theme-color\\" content=\\"#000000\\" media=\\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\\"> \\n<meta name=\\"theme-color\\" content=\\"#ffffff\\" media=\\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\\">"],[313,"Responsive design matters so that a site or application looks and works properly on devices with different screen sizes, from mobile phones to desktop computers. It improves the user experience and increases accessibility, making the interface convenient for everyone. It also helps maintain a single style and the same functionality on every device.","Responsive design is an approach to building interfaces that lets web pages adapt automatically to different screen sizes. It matters that a site is convenient for users regardless of whether they visit from a phone, a tablet or a computer. To achieve this, flexible grids, proportional elements, media queries and other CSS techniques are used in order to ensure the content displays correctly.\\nAn example of responsive design would be using media queries in CSS to change the placement of elements according to the screen\'s width.",null],[314,"CSS preprocessors such as Sass and Less let you use variables, nested rules, functions and mixins, which simplifies the creation and maintenance of styles. They encourage modularity and the reuse of code, which makes styles better structured and easier to change. Preprocessors also add the ability to use logical operations and loops, which is impossible in ordinary CSS.","CSS preprocessors extend the capabilities of standard CSS, adding functionality that makes code more powerful and convenient to work with. Using variables, for example, lets you set values such as colours or sizes once and reuse them throughout your code:\\n\\nBesides variables, preprocessors let you use nested selectors, which improves the readability of the code, and mixins for grouping repeated styles. This makes a project better structured and easy to scale. Nesting, mixins and functions help you write cleaner, more manageable code, especially on large projects.","// An example with Sass\\n$primary-color: #3498db;\\n.button {\\n  background-color: $primary-color;\\n  &:hover {\\n    background-color: darken($primary-color, 10%);\\n  }\\n}"],[315,"CSS Grid and Flexbox simplify the creation of responsive, complex layouts. Grid suits building two-dimensional layouts where you need to control both rows and columns, while Flexbox is ideal for one-dimensional layouts such as aligning elements in a row. Both tools let you control alignment, the distribution of space and responsiveness easily.","CSS Grid and Flexbox are powerful tools for creating layouts that have replaced many older methods such as the use of floats and tables. Flexbox is intended for one-dimensional layouts, which makes it ideal for aligning elements in a row or a column. It simplifies the control of properties such as centring, stretching and the even distribution of elements:\\n\\nCSS Grid, by contrast, works with two-dimensional layouts and lets you define a layout by controlling both rows and columns. This is especially useful for creating complex, responsive grids:\\n\\nUsing Grid and Flexbox in combination gives you flexibility in creating layouts, letting you control both the large and the small elements of an interface precisely.",".container {\\n  display: flex;\\n  justify-content: space-between;\\n  align-items: center;\\n}\\n\\n.grid {\\n   display: grid;\\n   grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);\\n   grid-template-rows: auto;\\n}"],[316,"`block` elements take up the whole width of their parent and start on a new line; `inline` elements sit on the same line and take up only the width they need; and `inline-block` combines the properties of both — it sits on the same line but lets you set a width and height. The difference between these types affects how a page is built and laid out.","CSS has three main types of element display: `block`, `inline` and `inline-block`. Elements of the `block` type (`<div>`, `<h1>`, for example) take up the whole available width and start on a new line. This means the following elements will be placed beneath them. Elements of the `inline` type (`<span>`, `<a>`, for example) sit on the same line and take up only the width their content requires, ignoring the width and height properties. Elements of the `inline-block` type combine the properties of both — they sit on a line, but their width and height can be set as on blocks:\\n\\nThese differences are important to understand when creating layouts and controlling the placement of the elements on a page, especially when different markup approaches have to be combined.","<div style=\\"display: inline-block; width: 100px; height: 50px;\\">\\n  Block\\n</div>"],[317,"The most popular CSS frameworks include Bootstrap, Foundation and Bulma. They provide ready-made styles and components for speeding development up. Knowing such frameworks lets you build responsive web pages faster.","CSS frameworks such as Bootstrap, Foundation and Bulma make the development of web interfaces easier by offering ready-made components and styles for standard interface elements such as buttons, forms and navigation. They also include responsive grids for building layouts, which makes them especially useful when creating a responsive design.\\nUsing a framework lets you cut development time while ensuring consistency and compatibility between browsers. Bootstrap, for example — one of the most popular frameworks — provides an easy-to-use 12-column grid and components such as modal windows and carousels, which can be customized as needed.",null],[318,"If a block\'s content exceeds its dimensions, by default it will extend beyond the block. This can be controlled with CSS properties such as `overflow`, which let you hide the overflow, add scrolling or resize the block automatically.","When a block\'s content overflows, by default it will extend beyond its boundaries, which can look unattractive or cause elements to overlap. To prevent this you can use the `overflow` property, which supports several values: `visible` (the default, where the content extends beyond the block), `hidden` (which hides the overflowing content), `scroll` (which adds scrolling) and `auto` (which adds scrolling if there is more content than the block\'s size). To create a scrollable block with a fixed height, for example, you can set:\\n\\nThis will add vertical scrolling if there turns out to be more than 100 pixels of content.","div {\\n  height: 100px;\\n  overflow: auto;\\n}"],[319,"To control overflow you can use the properties `overflow`, `text-overflow` and `white-space`. These properties let you hide the content, add scrolling or trim it properly.","Overflow can be controlled with several CSS properties:\\n\\n- `overflow` controls a block\'s overflow (`visible`, `hidden`, `scroll`, `auto`, for example).\\n\\n- `text-overflow` controls the overflow of text inside a block with `overflow: hidden` and can be used to trim text or add an ellipsis (`ellipsis`, for example).\\n\\n- `white-space` determines how whitespace and line breaks are handled (`nowrap` prevents breaks, for example). For text that does not fit on one line, a combination is often used:\\n\\nThis adds an ellipsis at the end of the line if the text exceeds the block\'s width.","div {\\n  white-space: nowrap;\\n  overflow: hidden;\\n  text-overflow: ellipsis;\\n}"],[320,"The CSS properties relating to content, padding, borders and margins include `width` and `height` for the content\'s dimensions, `padding` for the inner spacing, `border` for the borders and `margin` for the outer spacing. These properties control the size and placement of the elements on a page.","In CSS the boxes of elements consist of four main parts: the content, the padding, the border and the margin. The `width` and `height` properties determine the content\'s dimensions — that is, the space taken up by the text or other data inside the element.\\nThe `padding` property sets the inner spacing between the content and the border, making the element visually larger. `border` adds a visible frame around the element, and its thickness affects the element\'s overall size.\\nThe `margin` property sets the outer spacing, creating space around the element and determining the distance to the neighbouring elements. This model is called the box model, and understanding its principles lets you control the appearance and placement of the elements on a page precisely.",null],[321,"Margin collapsing happens when two vertical margins of neighbouring blocks combine into one, equal to the larger of them. Horizontal margins do not collapse, since collapsing affects only vertical spacing.","In CSS vertical margins can collapse — that is, combine into a single shared margin. This happens when two neighbouring blocks have vertical margins, and instead of adding them together the browser chooses the larger of the two. Collapsing can also happen with empty blocks and with the first and last child elements.\\nHorizontal margins, by contrast, do not collapse, since such spacing does not normally cause elements to overlap one another. If one element has `margin-bottom: 20px`, for example, and the next has `margin-top: 30px`, the total gap between them will be 30px rather than 50px.",null],[322,"CSS has several pseudo-classes you can use to control the states of elements, such as `:hover` (on hover), `:active` (while being pressed), `:focus` (when it receives focus), `:disabled` (for disabled elements) and `:checked` (for selected form elements). These states let you change elements\' styles according to the user\'s actions, creating a more interactive, responsive interface.","The states of elements in CSS let you change styles according to the user\'s interactions. The `:hover` pseudo-class, for example, is applied when the user hovers the cursor over an element, letting you change its appearance, which can be useful for buttons or links. The `:active` pseudo-class fires when an element is clicked — a button can change colour while being pressed, for example. `:focus` is used for form elements such as text fields, in order to mark them out when they receive focus from the keyboard or the mouse. `:disabled` is applied to form elements in order to show that they are inactive, while `:checked` is used for selected checkboxes or radio buttons.\\nBy using these states you can make an interface more convenient and comprehensible for users, adding interactive effects without JavaScript.\\nFor example:\\n\\nThese examples show how an element\'s appearance can be changed according to its state.","button:hover {\\n  background-color: lightblue;\\n}\\ninput:focus {\\n  border-color: blue;\\n}\\ninput:disabled {\\n  opacity: 0.5;\\n}\\n.checkbox:checked + label {\\n  font-weight: bold;\\n}"],[323,"In the BEM methodology:\\n\\"Block\\" is an independent component — a button or a menu, for example.\\n\\"Element\\" is a part of a block that cannot exist on its own — the text inside a button, for example.\\n\\"Modifier\\" is a state or version of a block or element — a button with an additional class for changing its colour, for example.","BEM is a methodology developed to simplify the writing and maintenance of code.\\n\\n- A block is an independent component of an interface. `button`, `form` or `header` can be blocks, for example. They may contain elements but can exist on their own.\\n\\n- An element is a constituent part of a block that makes sense only in the context of its block. `button__icon` or `form__input`, for example. Elements are marked by two underscores after the block\'s name.\\n\\n- A modifier is used to change the state of a block or an element. `button--active` or `form__input--error`, for example. It describes different variations or states of a block or element and is separated by two hyphens.\\n\\nUsing BEM lets you create a clear hierarchy of styles, which makes projects easier to maintain and scale.",null],[324,"The structure of CSS classes can be organized with methodologies such as BEM, SMACSS or OOCSS. It is important to use semantic class names that reflect their purpose, and to apply approaches such as modularity and reusability in order to avoid duplicated code. Styles should also be grouped by component or by page, in order to make navigating and understanding the code easier.","Organizing the structure of CSS classes matters for the maintainability and scalability of a project.\\nMethodologies such as BEM, SMACSS and OOCSS help you divide styles into components, which makes code better structured and more comprehensible.\\nIn BEM, for example, blocks are self-contained components that may have elements and modifiers. This lets you create reusable styles and reduces duplicated code.\\nIn addition, the use of mixins and functions in CSS preprocessors such as Sass or Less simplifies the creation and maintenance of styles.\\nMixins let you group repeated rules, while functions let you perform calculations and return values.\\nWith a mixin for a button, for example, you can configure its styles for different states easily.\\nCSS modules, applied in modern frameworks such as React, generate unique classes for every component automatically, which prevents name conflicts and isolates the styles. This approach makes code more manageable and improves the interaction between components.",null],[325,"The document flow in CSS is the order in which elements are displayed on a page. It determines how elements are placed relative to one another and how they affect the size and position of other elements. The flow can change according to the value of the `display` property and of other CSS properties such as `position`.","The document flow in CSS describes how the elements on a web page are placed. By default elements are placed in the \\"flow\\" from the top down and from left to right. There are several key aspects of the document flow:\\n\\n- The normal flow: this is the order in which elements appear in the HTML. Block elements are placed vertically, inline ones horizontally.\\n\\n- The effect of `display`: values of `display` such as `block`, `inline`, `inline-block`, `flex` and `grid` can change the normal flow. `flex` and `grid`, for example, let you change the position of elements without changing their order in the HTML.\\n\\n- The `position` properties: if an element has `position: absolute` or `position: fixed`, it is taken out of the normal flow and does not affect the position of the other elements. This can cause elements to overlap.\\n\\n- The flow of backgrounds and borders: although an element can be \\"pushed\\" out of the flow with `position`, its background colour and borders will still be visible where they were.\\n\\nUnderstanding the document flow matters for working successfully with markup and design, since it helps you create pages that are logically structured and easy to take in.",null],[326,"Pseudo-classes are keywords in CSS that add special states to selectors. `:hover`, for example, changes an element\'s style when the mouse cursor is over it. Other examples include `:focus`, which applies to input elements while they are active, and `:nth-child()`, which lets you select elements by their ordinal position. Pseudo-classes make styling interactions and states easier without any need to add extra classes to the HTML.","Pseudo-classes in CSS let you apply styles to elements based on their state, their position or other criteria, without requiring any change to the HTML. They begin with a colon and can be used in various scenarios. The main pseudo-classes include:\\n• `:hover`: applied when the cursor is over an element. You can change a button\'s background colour when the mouse is over it, for example.\\n• `:focus`: used for input elements while they are active. This is useful for improving accessibility.\\n• `:active`: applied while an element is actively being pressed — for buttons, for example.\\n• `:nth-child()`: it lets you select elements by their order. You can select every second item in a list, for example:","li:nth-child(2n) {\\n  background-color: lightgray;\\n}"],[327,"The `position` property determines how elements are positioned on a page. The main values include `static`, `relative`, `absolute`, `fixed` and `sticky`.\\nThe value `static` is the default, in which the element sits in the normal flow. `relative` lets you offset an element from its normal position, `absolute` positions an element relative to its nearest positioned ancestor, `fixed` fixes an element relative to the browser window, and `sticky` combines the behaviour of `relative` and `fixed`, keeping the element in place while the page scrolls.","The `position` property in CSS determines how elements are placed on a web page. Here are the main values and their characteristics:\\n\\n- `static`:\\n\\n- This is the default value.\\n\\n- The element sits in the document\'s normal flow.\\n\\n- The properties `top`, `right`, `bottom` and `left` cannot be used.\\n\\n- `relative`:\\n\\n- The element is placed relative to its normal position.\\n\\n- The properties `top`, `right`, `bottom` and `left` can be used to offset the element.\\n\\n- The element still occupies its place in the document\'s flow.\\n\\n- `absolute`:\\n\\n- The element is placed relative to its nearest positioned ancestor (one with `relative`, `absolute` or `fixed`, for example).\\n\\n- It occupies no place in the document\'s normal flow.\\n\\n- `fixed`:\\n\\n- The element is fixed relative to the browser window.\\n\\n- It stays in place while the page scrolls.\\n\\n- `sticky`:\\n\\n- The element behaves like `relative` until it reaches a given scroll position, after which it becomes fixed.\\n\\n- This is useful for creating navigation bars or headings that stay visible while the page scrolls.",null],[328,"An element can be centred horizontally and vertically in CSS in various ways. For block elements you can use Flexbox or Grid, setting `justify-content` and `align-items` to `center`.\\nFor elements with fixed dimensions you can use `position: absolute` with `top` and `left` set to 50%, and apply `transform: translate(-50%, -50%)`.","Centring elements in CSS can be done in several ways, depending on the context and the requirements. Here are the main methods:\\n\\n- With Flexbox:\\n\\nSet the property `display: flex` on the parent element, and then use `justify-content` for horizontal centring and `align-items` for vertical.\\n\\n- With CSS Grid:\\n\\nAs with Flexbox, with Grid you can centre elements easily using the `place-items` property.\\n\\n- With `position: absolute`:\\n\\nIf the element\'s size is known, it can be centred by setting `top` and `left` to 50% and using `transform`.\\n\\n• With `margin`:\\nFor block elements with a fixed width you can use automatic margins.\\n\\nThese methods let you centre elements effectively in various situations, improving visual harmony and the user experience.",".container {\\n  display: flex;\\n  justify-content: center; /* Horizontal centring */\\n  align-items: center;    /* Vertical centring */\\n  height: 100vh;         /* The container\'s height */\\n}\\n\\n.grid-container {\\n  display: grid;\\n  place-items: center; /* Centring in both directions */\\n  height: 100vh;\\n}\\n\\n.element {\\n  position: absolute;\\n  top: 50%;\\n  left: 50%;\\n  transform: translate(-50%, -50%); /* The offset to the centre */\\n}\\n\\n.block-element {\\n  width: 300px;      /* A fixed width */\\n  margin: 0 auto;    /* Horizontal centring */\\n}"],[329,"CSS has two main categories of unit: absolute and relative:\\n\\n- Absolute units include `px`, `cm` and `in`, which are fixed and do not depend on the context\\n\\n- Relative units such as `%`, `em`, `rem` and `vw` depend on other dimensions — the font size or the parent element\'s size, for example.\\n\\nUsing relative units lets you create more responsive, flexible layouts.","Units of measurement in CSS are used to set the dimensions, spacing, height, width and other properties of elements. Here are the main types of unit:\\n\\n- Absolute units:\\n\\n- `px`: pixels are a fixed unit of measurement. They are the most widespread and suit the precise control of elements\' dimensions.\\n\\n`cm`, `mm`, `in`: centimetres, millimetres and inches are used for printed documents but are rarely applied in web design, because they depend on the screen\'s resolution.\\n\\n- Relative units:\\n\\n- `%`: percentage units define a size relative to the parent element — the width of a child block as a share of its container, for example.\\n\\n- `em`: relative to the font size of the element itself (or of its parent, for properties that are not font-size).\\n\\n- `rem`: relative to the root element\'s font size (`html`), which makes it predictable throughout a document.\\n\\n- `vw` and `vh`: one hundredth of the viewport\'s width and height respectively, which is convenient for full-screen blocks.\\n\\nRelative units are preferable for responsive markup, since the layout then adapts to the font size and to the device\'s screen.",null],[330,"CSS selectors are patterns that determine which HTML elements will be styled.\\nThere are several kinds of selector:\\n\\n- simple selectors (by tag, class or identifier),\\n\\n- compound selectors (combining several simple ones),\\n\\n- pseudo-classes (:hover or :focus, for example)\\n\\n- pseudo-elements (::before and ::after, for example).\\n\\nYou can also use attribute selectors to pick elements by the values of their attributes. Understanding selectors lets you control the styles on a web page more precisely and more effectively.","Selectors in CSS are a powerful tool that lets developers pick out and style HTML elements. Here are the main kinds of selector:\\nSimple selectors:\\n\\n- The tag selector: it picks every element of a particular type.\\n\\n- The class selector: it picks the elements with the given class.\\n\\n- The identifier selector: it picks the element with a particular identifier.\\n\\nCompound selectors: they let you combine several simple selectors.\\nPseudo-classes: they let you pick elements based on their state — when the cursor is hovering over them, for example.\\nPseudo-elements: they let you style parts of an element — the first line of text, for example.\\nAttribute selectors: they pick elements based on the values of their attributes.\\nUnderstanding the various kinds of selector and how they are applied lets developers manage the styles of web pages effectively, creating responsive, interactive interfaces.",null],[331,"Keyframes are a CSS rule that defines the intermediate states of an animation. It lets you state how an element should look at the different stages of the animation.\\nUsing the `@keyframes` directive you can set styles at particular moments in time. The animation is then applied to an element through the `animation` property, where you name the keyframes and give other parameters such as the duration and the timing.","CSS keyframes are used to create animations, letting developers set an element\'s style at the various stages of the animation. The main steps for using keyframes are:\\n\\n- Defining the keyframes: an `@keyframes` rule is created, stating how the element\'s style should change at the different moments in time.\\n\\n- Applying the animation to an element: the `animation` property is used to apply the animation to an element, naming the keyframes and giving the duration and the timing.\\n\\n- Additional animation parameters: you can state properties such as `animation-delay` for a delay before the animation starts, and `animation-iteration-count` for setting the number of repetitions.\\n\\n- Using several keyframes: you can create more complex animations by defining several intermediate frames using percentages.\\n\\nAnimations using keyframes make interfaces more dynamic and attractive, adding interactive elements that improve the user experience.","@keyframes example {\\n  from {\\n    opacity: 0; /* The initial transparency */\\n    transform: translateY(-20px); /* The initial offset */\\n  }\\n  to {\\n    opacity: 1; /* The final transparency */\\n    transform: translateY(0); /* The final offset */\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n.fade-in {\\n  animation: example 1s ease-in-out; /* 1 second, a smooth start and finish */\\n}\\n\\n.fade-in {\\n  animation: example 1s ease-in-out 0.5s 2; /* 2 repetitions after a 0.5 second delay */\\n}\\n\\n@keyframes complex-animation {\\n  0% {\\n    opacity: 0;\\n    transform: translateY(-20px);\\n  }\\n  50% {\\n    opacity: 0.5;\\n    transform: translateY(10px);\\n  }\\n  100% {\\n    opacity: 1;\\n    transform: translateY(0);\\n  }\\n}"],[332,"The `border` property creates a frame around an element and occupies space in the document model, whereas `outline` also creates a frame but takes up no space and does not affect the element\'s dimensions. In addition, `outline` can be used only with particular properties, such as `outline-color`, `outline-style` and `outline-width`.","The `border` and `outline` properties in CSS are both used to create frames around elements, but they have several key differences:\\n\\n- The definition:\\n\\n- `border`: this property defines a frame around an element, which can be configured as to style, colour and width.\\n\\n- `outline`: this property creates an outline around an element that does not affect the element\'s dimensions or positioning.\\n\\n- The effect on the document model:\\n\\n- `border`: the frame takes up space, and its size affects the element\'s dimensions.\\n\\n- `outline`: the outline takes up no space and does not affect the element\'s dimensions.\\n\\n- Configuration:\\n\\n- `border`: it has a great many properties for configuration, letting you style the frame differently on each side.\\n\\n- `outline`: it consists of three main properties, which have to be defined together.\\n\\nThese differences let developers choose the appropriate property according to the design and functional requirements of a web page.",null],[333,"Pseudo-elements are special constructs in CSS that let you style particular parts of an element rather than the whole of it.\\nThe pseudo-elements `::before` and `::after`, for example, let you add content before or after an element\'s content. This is convenient for adding decorative elements or styles without changing the HTML markup. Pseudo-elements help improve the visual presentation of elements and make a design more flexible.","Pseudo-elements in CSS let developers add styles to particular parts of an element without changing the HTML code itself. The main pseudo-elements include:\\n\\n- `::before`: this pseudo-element adds content before an element\'s content. It is used to insert icons, text labels or other visual elements.\\n\\n- `::after`: this pseudo-element adds content after an element\'s content. It is also useful for adding decorative elements or icons.\\n\\n- Other pseudo-elements: there are also `::first-line`, which styles the first line of text, and `::first-letter`, which applies styles to the first letter.\\n\\nPseudo-elements let developers improve the visual style of pages without making changes to the HTML, which makes them a powerful tool for designers. They increase flexibility and make the creation of complex visual effects easier.",".example::before {\\n  content: \\"→ \\";\\n  color: blue;\\n}\\n\\n.example::after {\\n  content: \\" ←\\";\\n  color: red;\\n}"],[334,"The `background-size` property in CSS determines the dimensions of an element\'s background image. The main values of this property include:\\n\\n- `auto` (the image keeps its original dimensions),\\n\\n- `cover` (the image is scaled so as to cover the element entirely), and\\n\\n- `contain` (the image is scaled so as to fit inside the element entirely, keeping its proportions). Understanding these values helps you create more flexible, responsive designs.","The `background-size` property controls the dimensions of a background image and has several values, each intended for a different scenario of use:\\n\\n- `auto`: this value keeps the background image\'s original dimensions. It is used when the image has to be displayed unchanged, but it can mean the image does not fill the whole element.\\n\\n- `cover`: this value scales the image so that it fills the element entirely, keeping its proportions. The image may be cropped if its dimensions do not match the element\'s.\\n\\n- `contain`: this value scales the image so that it fits inside the element entirely, also keeping its proportions. In this case empty areas may remain if the aspect ratios of the image and the element differ.\\n\\nThese values help developers manage background images effectively, improving the appearance and functionality of web pages. The choice of the right value depends on the design\'s goals and the context of use.",".element {\\n  background-image: url(\'image.jpg\');\\n  background-size: auto;\\n}\\n\\n.element {\\n  background-image: url(\'image.jpg\');\\n  background-size: cover;\\n}\\n\\n.element {\\n  background-image: url(\'image.jpg\');\\n  background-size: contain;\\n}"],[335,"To enlarge an element on hover without shifting its neighbours, you can use the `transform` property with the value `scale()`. It is important to set `overflow: hidden` on the parent element in order to hide the parts of the enlarged element that stick out. You can also use `transition` so that the change of size animates smoothly.","To create the effect of an element enlarging on hover without shifting its neighbours, you can use a combination of the `transform` and `transition` properties. The main steps are:\\n\\n- Using `transform`: this property lets you scale an element with the `scale()` function. `scale(1.2)`, for example, will enlarge the element by 20 %.\\n\\n- Setting `overflow` on the parent: to avoid shifting the neighbouring elements during the enlargement, you can set `overflow: hidden` on the parent container. This will hide any part of the element that extends beyond it.\\n\\n- Adding smoothness with `transition`: the `transition` property lets you make the changes smoother by setting the duration and the easing of the animation.\\n\\nThis approach helps you create the effect of an element enlarging to draw attention without disturbing the placement of the other elements on the page.",".element {\\n  transition: transform 0.3s ease;\\n}\\n.element:hover {\\n  transform: scale(1.2);\\n}\\n\\n.container {\\n  overflow: hidden;\\n}"],[336,"The `text-transform` property in CSS is used to change the case of text. It lets you convert text to upper case (uppercase) or lower case (lowercase), or capitalize the first letter of every word (capitalize). This property is useful for styling text without changing its original HTML code.","The `text-transform` property in CSS controls the display of a text\'s case, offering several options for styling:\\n\\n- `uppercase`: it converts every letter to upper case.\\n\\n- `lowercase`: it converts every letter to lower case.\\n\\n- `capitalize`: it converts the first letter of every word to upper case.\\n\\n- `none`: it states that the text\'s case must not be changed.\\n\\n- `text-transform` lets developers control the visual presentation of text on web pages effectively. This is especially useful for achieving consistency in a design or for improving readability, without making any change to the text itself.",null],[337,"`!important` is a special rule in CSS that lets you override any other styles, even those with higher specificity. When !important is added to a property, that property takes priority and any other rules for the element are ignored.\\nUsing !important is not recommended, however, since it can make code harder to maintain and lead to confusion about which styles apply.","`!important` is used in CSS to raise the priority of a particular rule, letting it override any other rule even if that rule has higher specificity. It can be used like this:\\n\\nUsing `!important` should be approached with care, however. Here are several key aspects:\\n\\n- Priority: CSS processes styles in order of specificity. If two rules apply to the same element, the rule with the higher specificity takes priority. If one of the rules is marked !important, however, it will override the others regardless of their specificity.\\n\\n- Difficulty of maintenance: excessive use of !important can make code harder to read and maintain. Developers reading the code may get confused trying to work out which styles apply and where they are defined.\\n\\n- Best practices: instead of using !important you should aim at a clearer organization of your styles. That may involve using more specific selectors or refactoring the styles in order to eliminate conflicts. If you often need !important, that may be a sign that your CSS\'s structure needs improving.\\n\\nThe use of !important should be justified, and it should be avoided where possible, in order to keep code clean and predictable.",".example {\\n  color: blue !important; /* This rule will be applied regardless of the others */\\n}"],[338,"CSS has four main selector combinators:\\n\\n- descendant (` `),\\n\\n- child (`>`),\\n\\n- adjacent sibling (`+`)\\n\\n- general sibling (`~`).\\n\\nThe descendant combinator picks elements that are inside another element, not necessarily at the first level.\\nThe child combinator picks only direct children.\\nThe adjacent sibling combinator picks the element that comes immediately after another element, while the general sibling combinator picks every element that comes after another element, regardless of its nesting level.","Selector combinators in CSS let developers pick elements for styling more precisely. Let us look at each of them:\\n\\n- Descendant (` `): the descendant combinator picks elements that are inside another element, regardless of the nesting level. The selector `div p`, for example, applies styles to every paragraph inside a `div` element.\\n\\n- Child (`>`): this combinator picks only direct children. `ul > li`, for example, will pick only those `li` elements that are direct children of a `ul` element.\\n\\n- Adjacent (`+`): the adjacent sibling selector picks the element that comes immediately after another element. `h1 + p`, for example, will pick the first paragraph that comes immediately after an `h1` heading.\\n\\n- General sibling (`~`): this combinator picks every element that follows the given element at the same nesting level. `h1 ~ p`, for example, will pick every paragraph that comes after an `h1` heading, however many there are.","div p {\\n  color: blue; /* Every <p> inside a <div> will be blue */\\n}\\n\\nul > li {\\n  list-style-type: none; /* Only the direct child <li> elements will have no markers */\\n}\\n\\nh1 + p {\\n  margin-top: 0; /* It removes the top margin from the first <p> after an <h1> */\\n}\\n\\nh1 ~ p {\\n  color: green; /* Every <p> after an <h1> will be green */\\n}"],[339,"To create a shadow falling from a block, the `box-shadow` property in CSS is used. It takes values for the offset along the X axis, the offset along the Y axis, the blur, the spread and the shadow\'s colour.\\nThe value `box-shadow: 2px 2px 5px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5);`, for example, will create a shadow offset 2 pixels to the right and down, with a 5-pixel blur.","The `box-shadow` property in CSS lets developers add shadows to elements, creating an effect of depth and realism. It takes the following parameters:\\n\\n- The offset along the X axis: it determines how far the shadow is offset horizontally from the element. A positive value moves the shadow to the right, a negative one to the left.\\n\\n- The offset along the Y axis: it determines how far the shadow is offset vertically from the element. A positive value moves the shadow down, a negative one up.\\n\\n- The blur: it determines the degree to which the shadow is blurred. The larger the value, the more blurred the shadow.\\n\\n- The spread (an optional parameter): it determines how far the shadow will be expanded. A positive value expands the shadow, a negative one contracts it.\\n\\nThe colour: it determines the shadow\'s colour; RGBA is most often used, in order to add transparency.\\nAn example of using `box-shadow`:",".box {\\n  box-shadow: 2px 2px 5px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5);\\n}"],[340,"`drop-shadow()` is a CSS function used to add a shadow to elements, images included. It is applied through the `filter` property and takes values for the offset along the X axis, the offset along the Y axis, the blur and the shadow\'s colour. This function is especially useful because it lets you preserve an image\'s transparent areas, creating a natural, tidy shadow effect.","The `drop-shadow()` function is part of the CSS specification and is used to add a shadow to elements with a transparent background. It is ideally suited to images, since it can give them a more striking appearance. Unlike the traditional `box-shadow` property, which applies to the whole box, `drop-shadow()` focuses on the element\'s visible pixels, which avoids an unwanted overlay on the transparent areas.\\nThe syntax:\\n\\n- offset-x: the shadow\'s horizontal offset.\\n\\n- offset-y: the shadow\'s vertical offset.\\n\\n- blur-radius: the radius of the shadow\'s blur.\\n\\n- color: the shadow\'s colour.\\n\\nAn example of use:\\n\\nIn this example the shadow will be offset 5 pixels to the right and down, with a blur radius of 10 pixels and a semi-transparent black colour.\\nThe `drop-shadow()` function can also be combined with other filters such as `blur()` or `brightness()` in order to create more complex visual effects.","filter: drop-shadow(offset-x offset-y blur-radius color);\\n\\nimg {\\n  filter: drop-shadow(5px 5px 10px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5));\\n}"],[341,"To rotate a block by 45 degrees in CSS the `transform` property with the `rotate()` function is used. This lets you change an element\'s orientation relative to its initial position. The value `rotate(45deg)`, for example, will rotate the element 45 degrees clockwise.","To rotate an element by 45 degrees, the CSS property `transform` is used. This property lets you apply various transformations such as rotation, scaling and translation. In the case of rotation, the `rotate()` function takes an angle in degrees or radians.\\nThe syntax:\\n\\n- angle: the angle of rotation, expressed in degrees (deg) or radians (rad).\\n\\nTo rotate an element by 45 degrees, for example, you write:\\n\\nIt is important to remember that when transformations are used, an element\'s position may change. For correct positioning you can use the `transform-origin` property, which determines the point the rotation happens around. By default this is the element\'s centre.",".selector {\\n  transform: rotate(angle);\\n}\\n\\n.box {\\n  transform: rotate(45deg);\\n}"],[342,"To make an animation repeat endlessly, the `animation` property is used with the value `infinite`. This value states that the animation has to run without stopping as long as the element is visible.\\n`animation: example 2s infinite;`, for example, will repeat the animation named `example` every 2 seconds.","Animations in CSS are created with keyframes (`@keyframes`), which describe how an element should change over time. For an animation to run endlessly, the value `infinite` has to be given in the `animation` property.\\nAn example of creating an endlessly repeating animation:\\n\\nIn this example the element will move smoothly upwards and return to its original place every second. The animation\'s parameters can be configured by changing the duration and other properties such as `ease-in` and `ease-out` for controlling the animation\'s speed.","@keyframes example {\\n  from { transform: translateY(0); }\\n  to { transform: translateY(-20px); }\\n}\\n.box {\\n  animation: example 1s infinite;\\n}"],[343,"To remove the underline from hyperlinks in CSS, the `text-decoration` property with the value `none` is used. This removes the standard underline that browsers add to `<a>` elements.\\nFor example, you can write:\\n\\n`a {\\ntext-decoration: none;\\n}`","Hyperlinks in HTML are underlined by default, which helps users recognize them. In some cases, however, that underline has to be removed in order to improve a page\'s design.\\nThe syntax:\\n\\nThis rule will remove the underline from every hyperlink on the page. If you need to remove the underline only from particular links, you can use classes or identifiers.\\nIt is also worth remembering that it is important to keep links readable and accessible, so it is better to add other visual cues — a change of colour on hover, for example.","a {\\n  text-decoration: none;\\n}"],[344,"The `float` property is used to control the placement of elements in the document\'s flow. It lets elements \\"float\\" to the left or the right, letting text and other elements wrap around them. The main values are `left`, `right`, `none` and `inherit`.","The `float` property controls the placement of elements, letting text wrap around them, which is useful for creating complex layouts. When an element is given a `float` value, it pushes itself out of the document\'s normal flow, and the elements around it can adapt to that change.\\nThe main values of `float`:\\n\\n- left: the element will sit on the left, and text will wrap around it on the right.\\n\\n- right: the element will sit on the right, and text will wrap around it on the left.\\n\\n- none: the element will not float and will stay in the document\'s normal flow.\\n\\n- inherit: the element inherits the `float` value from its parent element.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nIn this example the image will sit on the left while the text wraps around it on the right. Using `float` can lead to problems with alignment, however, so a `clearfix` is often necessary in order to clear the float and avoid unwanted effects in the layout.",".img {\\n  float: left;\\n  margin: 10px;\\n}"],[345,"Gradients in CSS can be added with the `background-image` property and the `linear-gradient()` or `radial-gradient()` functions.\\n\\n- `linear-gradient()` creates a transition between colours along a straight line\\n\\n- `radial-gradient()` creates a radial transition emanating from a centre.\\n\\nGradients can be used to create background images, to fill elements and for various visual effects","Gradients in CSS let you create smooth transitions between two or more colours, which adds depth and texture to the elements on a web page. There are two main kinds of gradient: linear and radial.\\n\\n- Linear gradients: used to create colour transitions along a straight line.\\nbackground-image: linear-gradient(direction, color-stop1, color-stop2, ...);\\n\\n- direction: the gradient\'s direction (`to right`, `to bottom`, `45deg`, for example).\\n\\n- color-stop: the colours the transition happens between.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nIn this example the gradient will go from red to blue, from left to right.\\n\\n- Radial gradients: they create a radial transition from a centre.\\n\\n- shape: the gradient\'s shape (`circle` or `ellipse`).\\n\\n- size: the gradient\'s size (`closest-side`, `farthest-corner`, for example).\\n\\n- position: the position of the gradient\'s centre.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nIn this example the gradient will go from yellow in the centre to green at the edges.\\nGradients can be combined with other properties such as `background-size`, `background-repeat` and `opacity` in order to create more complex visual effects. It is also important to remember that gradients can be used not only for backgrounds but also for borders, text and other elements, which makes them a versatile tool in web design.",".element {\\n  background-image: linear-gradient(to right, red, blue);\\n}\\n\\nbackground-image: radial-gradient(shape size at position, color-stop1, color-stop2, ...);\\n\\n.element {\\n  background-image: radial-gradient(circle, yellow, green);\\n}"],[346,"Inline CSS is applied directly to HTML elements through the `style` attribute; internal CSS is placed inside a `<style>` tag in the document\'s `<head>` section; and external CSS is attached using a `.css` file, which is linked through a `<link>` tag.","CSS has three ways of adding styles to a web page: inline, internal and external. Each of these approaches has its own characteristics and is applied in different situations.\\n\\n- Inline CSS:\\nIt defines styles directly in HTML tags using the `style` attribute.\\n\\nIt suits small changes or testing styles, but it makes code harder to maintain, since the styles are scattered throughout the document.\\n\\n- Internal CSS:\\nIt is added in the `<head>` section of an HTML document inside a `<style>` tag.\\n\\nIt suits small projects, or cases where the styles have to apply to one page only. If the styles are used on several pages, however, it can lead to duplicated code.\\n\\n- External CSS:\\nIt is attached using a separate `.css` file, which is linked to the HTML document through a `<link>` tag in the `<head>` section.\\n\\nIt is the most preferable approach for medium and large projects, since it lets you manage styles centrally, improves the readability of the code and makes maintenance easier.\\n\\nEach method can be used depending on a project\'s needs. On large projects external CSS is preferred, since it makes maintaining and reusing styles easier. Inline styles are best suited to testing and quick prototyping, while internal styles are convenient for small pages with unique styling requirements.","<p style=\\"color: red; font-size: 16px;\\">Text with an inline style</p>\\n\\n<head>\\n<style>    \\n  p {      \\n    color: red;      \\n    font-size: 16px;    \\n  }  \\n</style> \\n</head>\\n\\n<head>  \\n <link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"styles.css\\">\\n</head>"],[347,"In CSS the order in which pseudo-classes are applied matters, especially when several pseudo-classes can apply to the same element at once. To define the styles correctly in such situations, there is the LVHA order: `:link`, `:visited`, `:hover`, `:active`.\\nThis order helps ensure the styles are displayed correctly for interactive elements such as links.","In CSS it is important to consider the order in which pseudo-classes are applied, since an element may satisfy several pseudo-classes at once. A link may match both the `:hover` and the `:active` pseudo-class, for example, if the user clicks it without moving the cursor away.\\nTo ensure the styles are applied correctly, the LVHA order is used:\\n\\n- `:link` (a normal link),\\n\\n- `:visited` (a visited link),\\n\\n- `:hover` (a link under the cursor),\\n\\n- `:active` (a link while being clicked).\\n\\nThis order helps prevent style conflicts and makes the interface more predictable for users.",null],[348,"CSS media queries support various media types, such as:\\n\\n- `screen` (for screens),\\n\\n- `print` (for printing),\\n\\n- `tv` (for televisions),\\n\\n- `braille` (for devices with tactile output),\\n\\n- `tty` (for devices with a fixed character width),\\n\\n- `embossed` (for devices that print embossed Braille).\\n\\nThese media types let you adapt your styles according to the kind of device or environment.","CSS media queries support a great many media types, which let you style content according to the device used to display it:\\n\\n- `screen` — intended for the screens of computers, smartphones and tablets. It is the most widespread media type.\\n\\n- `print` — applied to printed versions of documents, so that the styles on the page look right when printed.\\n\\n- `tv` — used for devices with television screens. It normally implies large screen sizes and a low resolution compared with computers.\\n\\n- `braille` — intended for devices with tactile output, which use Braille to reproduce text.\\n\\n- `tty` — for devices with a fixed character width, such as teletypes and terminals.\\n\\n- `embossed` — used for devices that print on paper with embossed Braille.\\n\\n- `speech` — for speech synthesizers, which read text aloud.\\n\\nUsing media queries helps you adapt web content for various devices and conditions, which matters especially for ensuring accessibility and ease of use.",null],[349,"CSS sprites are a method of combining several images into a single file in order to reduce the number of HTTP requests when a page loads.\\nUsing coordinates and dimensions, CSS lets you display individual parts of that shared image as though they were separate pictures.","CSS sprites combine several small images into one large one in order to reduce the number of requests to the server. When a web page loads, every request for an image takes time, which can slow its display down. Using a sprite, the browser loads one image instead of many, and with CSS styles (the `background-image`, `background-position` and `background-size` properties) only the parts of the sprite that are needed are shown.\\nYou can use sprites for icons, buttons or other repeated graphical elements, for example, in order to improve performance.",null],[350,"The `z-index` property controls the stacking order of elements along the Z axis (depth). The greater the z-index value, the closer the element is to the user. z-index works only for elements with a position of relative, absolute or fixed.","`z-index` is used to control the stacking order of the elements on a web page. The values may be either positive or negative, determining how close to or far from the user an element will be displayed.\\nIf several elements overlap, the one with the greater z-index has the higher priority and will end up on top of the others. By default elements with the same z-index value are stacked in the order they appear in the HTML.\\nThe z-index property works only when a position has been set (relative, absolute, fixed), since it relates to layered positioning.",null],[351,"The `background-repeat` property lets you control how background images are repeated on a page. Its values may be:\\n\\n- `repeat` (repetition horizontally and vertically),\\n\\n- `repeat-x` (repetition horizontally only)\\n\\n- `repeat-y` (repetition vertically only)\\n\\n- `no-repeat` (no repetition)","The `background-repeat` property determines how background images are repeated on a web page.\\nBy default background images are repeated both horizontally and vertically (`repeat`). You can restrict the repetition to one axis only with the values `repeat-x` (horizontally only) or `repeat-y` (vertically only).\\nIf you need the image to be displayed only once, the value `no-repeat` is used. This property is useful for creating background patterns or adding visual elements such as watermarks.",null],[352,"The `background-attachment` property is used to control the scrolling of a background image. It determines whether the background image will scroll along with the page (`scroll`) or stay in place (`fixed`).","The `background-attachment` property controls a background image\'s behaviour as the page scrolls. It can take several values:\\n\\n- `scroll` — the background scrolls along with the page (this is the default value).\\n\\n- `fixed` — the background stays in place even as the page\'s content scrolls.\\n\\n- `local` — the background scrolls along with the element, if it has scrollable content.\\n\\nThis property lets you create parallax effects, in which the background moves more slowly than the content, or create a sense that the background is static.",null],[353,"CSS provides several properties for controlling fonts, including:\\n\\n- `font-family`,\\n\\n- `font-size`\\n\\n- `font-weight`\\n\\n- `font-style`\\n\\n- `font-variant`,\\n\\n- `line-height`\\n\\nThese properties let you set the font family, the size, the style (italic or bold, for example), the line height and other parameters affecting the appearance of text.","There are several CSS attributes used to configure and style fonts:\\n\\n- `font-family`: it defines the font family used for the text. You can name several fonts so that the browser picks one that is available.\\n\\n- `font-size`: it sets the text\'s size. It can be given in pixels, percentages, em, rem or other units.\\n\\n- `font-weight`: it controls the font\'s thickness. It can take values from `100` to `900`, and also keywords such as `normal` or `bold`.\\n\\n- `font-style`: it defines the font\'s style — `normal`, `italic` or `oblique`, for example.\\n\\n- `font-variant`: it lets you set special variations of a font, such as `small-caps`.\\n\\n- `line-height`: it sets the height of a line and affects the spacing between lines.\\n\\n- `letter-spacing`: it controls the spacing between letters.\\n\\n- `word-spacing`: it determines the spacing between words.\\n\\n- `text-transform`: it converts text to upper or lower case, or capitalizes every word.\\n\\n- `font-stretch`: it lets you expand or condense a font, if the font supports it.\\n\\nThese properties give you complete control over the appearance of the text on a page, letting you adapt it to different devices and preferences.",null],[354,"To give an image or an element rounded corners, the CSS property `border-radius` is used. The value is given in pixels, percentages or other units, determining how rounded the corners will be.\\n`border-radius: 10px`, for example, will create rounded corners with a radius of 10 pixels.","The `border-radius` property in CSS lets you set the radius with which an element\'s corners are rounded. The greater the value, the more rounded the corners. This property can be used on both square and rectangular elements, images, buttons, blocks and other HTML elements included.\\n\\n- Rounding every corner equally: by giving a single value you can set the radius for every corner at once — `border-radius: 15px`, for example, will round every corner of the element by 15 pixels.\\n\\n- Rounding individual corners: you can give different values for each corner — `border-radius: 10px 20px 30px 40px`, for example, where the values go in the order: top left, top right, bottom right, bottom left.\\n\\n- Rounding with percentages: if you use percentage values — `border-radius: 50%`, for example — that will give the element a completely round shape, provided its width and height are equal.\\n\\nThe `border-radius` property lets you create modern interfaces with soft edges and rounded elements easily.",null],[355,"The browser determines which elements match a CSS selector by analysing the document\'s HTML structure and applying the selectors to every element. The process involves checking the selectors against the rules of specificity and then applying the styles it has found to the matching elements. The browser walks the DOM tree, comparing elements with the selectors in the styles until it finds matches.","The browser uses several stages to determine which elements match a given CSS selector:\\n\\n- Parsing the HTML:\\nWhen a page loads, the browser first takes the HTML code apart, creating a data structure known as the DOM tree (Document Object Model). This tree is a hierarchy of the elements contained in the page.\\n\\n- Parsing the CSS:\\nThe browser then takes the CSS code apart, creating a data structure known as the CSSOM (CSS Object Model). This structure contains every style rule defined for the various selectors.\\n\\n- Matching the selectors:\\nThe browser walks over every element in the DOM tree and compares it with the selectors from the CSSOM. This happens according to the following algorithm:\\n\\n- • The browser checks whether the element matches the selector by type (tags, classes, identifiers, for example).\\n\\n- • If the selector contains pseudo-classes (`:hover`, for example), the browser checks the element\'s state in order to determine whether the style applies.\\n\\n- • If the selector is a combined one (`div > p`, for example), the browser analyses the hierarchy of elements in order to check the match.\\n\\n- Specificity:\\nThe browser computes the specificity of every selector in order to determine which style to apply when several selectors may match the same element. Specificity is based on the type of selector (identifier, class, tag and so on) and determines the priority with which styles are applied.\\n\\n- Applying the styles:\\nOnce a selector has been matched to an element, the browser applies the corresponding styles, creating the page\'s final visual appearance. If several styles have been assigned to an element, the order in which they appear in the CSS and the specificity of the selectors are taken into account.\\n\\nThe browser therefore uses a sequence of steps to match selectors to HTML elements, which lets it apply styles precisely to every element on a page.",null],[356,"The `translate()` function is used when you need to offset an element without changing its place in the document\'s flow. This is useful for creating animations, or when the element\'s effect on the other elements has to be preserved. Absolute positioning, by contrast, takes an element out of the document\'s flow, which can cause unwanted effects such as overlapping other elements.","The `translate()` function and absolute positioning in CSS have different uses and effects:\\n\\n- Preserving the document\'s flow:\\n`translate()` lets you move an element on the screen while keeping its place in the document\'s flow. This means the neighbouring elements carry on being placed as though the element had not been moved. This matters for maintaining a layout and preventing elements from overlapping. If you have a button that needs to shift slightly on hover, for example, using `translate()` will be better, since it will not affect the placement of the neighbouring elements.\\n\\n- Animation and transformations:\\n`translate()` suits animations and transformations well, since it changes an element\'s visual presentation without changing its actual position in the document. This lets you apply effects such as smooth movement without having to change other styles or structures. An animation on hovering over a button, for example, can be implemented with `translate()` in order to shift the button.\\n\\n- Absolute positioning:\\nIt is used when an element has to be placed precisely relative to its parent container or the viewport. Absolute positioning takes the element out of the document\'s flow, which can lead to other elements being overlapped and require additional configuration in order to avoid placement problems. If an element has to overlap other elements or sit in a specific place, absolute positioning is better.\\n\\n- Performance:\\nIn some cases `translate()` can be more performant, since many browsers optimize graphical transformations, letting them use hardware acceleration. This can improve the smoothness of animations and reduce the rendering load.\\n\\nThe choice between `translate()` and absolute positioning therefore depends on the context. `translate()` suits offsetting elements without affecting the document\'s flow, and animations, while absolute positioning should be used when an element has to be placed precisely relative to other elements or to the viewport.",null],[357,"Adaptive web design (AWD) uses several fixed layouts for different screen sizes, ensuring an optimal display on each category of device. The mobile-first strategy begins by designing for mobile devices and then adds features and elements for larger screens. This means the main focus is on mobile users, whereas adaptive design tries to cover every possible device.","The difference between adaptive web design (AWD) and the mobile-first strategy lies in their approaches to designing and developing websites:\\nAdaptive web design (AWD):\\n\\n- The definition: adaptive web design uses several fixed layouts corresponding to particular screen sizes. This means the site has predefined breakpoints, and when the screen\'s size changes the appropriate layout is loaded.\\n\\n- How it works: a separate layout is created for every breakpoint, which lets you control the placement of elements on different devices precisely — mobile phones, tablets and desktops.\\n\\n- The advantages: it lets designers control the appearance and functionality completely on every device. AWD can provide an excellent user experience (UX) on every device, but it requires more time and effort in order to maintain several layouts.\\n\\nThe mobile-first strategy:\\n\\n- The definition: this approach begins the design with mobile devices and then adds more complex features and elements for tablets and desktops. A basic version of the site for mobile users is created first and is then extended for other devices.\\n\\n- How it works: additional styles for wider screens are added with media queries. This ensures the site\'s main features stay available on mobile devices, while extra elements can easily be switched on for a larger screen.\\n\\n- The advantages: mobile-first lets you build a minimalist, fast interface that loads quickly and is optimized for mobile users. This matters especially given the growing use of mobile devices. The approach also makes the code easier to maintain, since the site\'s main version is always ready for mobile users.\\n\\nIn conclusion, the choice between adaptive web design and the mobile-first strategy depends on the project\'s goals and its audience. The adaptive approach suits ensuring the integrity of a design on every device, while the mobile-first strategy puts the mobile experience first, which may be more relevant in modern web development.",null],[358,"Vendor prefixes are prefixes added to CSS properties and values in order to provide support for new features in various browsers.\\n`-webkit-` for WebKit-based browsers (Chrome, Safari), `-moz-` for Firefox and `-ms-` for Internet Explorer, for example.\\nUsing prefixes lets developers experiment with new properties before they are finally standardized and widely adopted.","Vendor prefixes are special prefixes added to CSS properties and values in order to provide support for new or experimental features in various browsers. Every browser normally has its own \\"vendor\\", and the prefixes let them implement their own versions of new properties before those become standard.\\nExamples of vendor prefixes:\\n\\n- -webkit-: used in WebKit-based browsers such as Google Chrome and Safari. An example:\\n\\n- -moz-: used in the Firefox browser.\\n\\n- -ms-: used in Internet Explorer and Edge.\\n\\n- -o-: used in Opera.\\n\\nWhy are vendor prefixes used?\\n\\n- Compatibility: they let developers use new features that are not yet supported by every browser.\\n\\n- Testing: developers can test experimental properties without waiting for them to be implemented fully in browsers.\\n\\n- Ensuring a correct display: in some cases, without the prefixes, browsers may display elements incorrectly or fail to support particular features.\\n\\nModern practice:\\nSince the CSS specifications have become more stable and browsers have begun supporting new properties, the use of vendor prefixes has become less widespread. They are still relevant for some features, however, such as animations and transformations. To manage prefixes you can use tools such as Autoprefixer, which add the necessary prefixes to CSS code automatically based on the properties used.\\nVendor prefixes therefore play an important part in the process of adopting new technologies in web development, ensuring compatibility and functionality across different browsers.","example {\\n  -webkit-transition: all 0.3s ease; /* The transition for WebKit */\\n  -moz-border-radius: 10px; /* Rounded corners for Firefox */\\n  -ms-flex: 1; /* The flex property for IE */\\n  -o-animation: fade 1s; /* The animation for Opera */\\n}"],[359,"These properties let you increment counters for elements such as headings, lists and others dynamically, which makes numbering more flexible and manageable.\\nWith `counter-reset` we create a new counter, and with `counter-increment` we increase its value for particular elements. We can use these properties to number the headings on a page automatically, for example. A counter can be reset with counter-reset, and every time an element with counter-increment appears the counter will be increased.","To create automatic numbering on a web page with CSS, we can use the `counter-reset` and `counter-increment` properties. These properties let us manage counters, which makes it possible to add numbers to elements automatically.\\nThe HTML structure:\\n\\nThe CSS styles:\\n\\nHow it works:\\n\\n- In the parent element `.section` we use `counter-reset: section;` in order to start a new counter for the sections.\\n\\n- Every time a second-level heading `<h2>` appears we increase the `section` counter with `counter-increment: section;`.\\n\\n- In the `::before` pseudo-element we display the counter\'s current value before the heading\'s content.\\n\\n- In the same way we can create a separate counter for subsections, in order to display numbers in the form \\"1.1\\", \\"1.2\\" and so on.\\n\\nWith this approach the headings on a page will be numbered automatically, which simplifies the structure considerably and improves navigation through the content.","<div class=\\"section\\">\\n  <h2>Section 1</h2>\\n  <p>Some text in section 1.</p>\\n  <h2>Section 2</h2>\\n  <p>Some text in section 2.</p>\\n  <h3>Subsection 2.1</h3>\\n  <p>Some text in subsection 2.1.</p>\\n  <h3>Subsection 2.2</h3>\\n  <p>Some text in subsection 2.2.</p>\\n</div>\\n\\n/* We set up the counter for the sections */\\n.section {\\n  counter-reset: section; /* We reset the counter to zero */\\n}\\n/* We number the second-level headings */\\nh2 {\\n  counter-increment: section; /* We increase the counter for every h2 */\\n}\\n/* We display the number before the heading */\\nh2::before {\\n  content: \\"Section \\" counter(section) \\". \\"; /* We print the section\'s number */\\n  font-weight: bold; /* We make the number bold */\\n}\\n/* We number the subsections (h3) */\\nh3 {\\n  counter-increment: subsection; /* We increase a separate counter for the h3 elements */\\n}\\n/* We display the number before the subheading */\\nh3::before {\\n  content: counter(section) \\".\\" counter(subsection) \\" \\"; /* We print the number including the parent section */\\n  font-weight: bold; /* We make the number bold */\\n}"],[360,"Splitting CSS files means dividing the styles into several files, each of which is responsible for a particular part of a web page or a component. This lets you manage the styles more easily, improves the readability of the code and helps the page load faster, since only the styles that are needed are loaded depending on the context. This approach makes code more modular and makes teamwork on a project easier.","Splitting CSS files is the practice of organizing a web page\'s styles into several separate files instead of keeping every style in one large file. This method has several key advantages:\\n\\n- Better modularity: dividing the styles by component or by page (separate files for navigation, forms, cards and so on, for example) lets developers find and edit the styles they need more easily. Every file can contain only the styles relating to one part of the application, which makes it more manageable.\\n\\n- Easier teamwork: when several developers work on a project, splitting the styles lets them work on different parts of the application without causing conflicts in the code. Each can add or change styles in their own file without touching the others.\\n\\n- Optimized loading: in some cases you can load styles asynchronously, only as they are needed. If a particular component is used on only one page, for example, its styles can be loaded only when the user goes to that page. This reduces the initial loading time and the overall volume of data sent to the user.\\n\\n- Better maintainability: when the styles are broken into files, the code is easier to maintain and update. Developers can find and change the styles they need quickly, avoiding the need to look through the whole CSS file. This matters especially on large projects, where the number of styles can be enormous.\\n\\nAn example of splitting the files:\\n\\nIn this example the files are organized so that each one is responsible for a particular part of the application\'s styles. When build systems such as Webpack or Gulp are used, these files can be combined into one file in order to optimize loading on the production server.","/css\\n    ├── main.css          // The main styles\\n    ├── layout.css        // The styles for the layout\\n    ├── components.css     // The styles for the individual components\\n    └── themes.css        // Themes and style variations"],[361,"Functions and mixins are used in CSS preprocessors to create reusable code. Mixins let you group styles and apply them to various elements, while functions perform calculations and return values that can be used in other styles.\\nThis helps reduce the amount of duplicated code and makes styles more manageable.","Functions and mixins are important concepts in CSS preprocessors such as Sass and LESS, and they help improve the organization and reuse of styles.\\nMixins\\nMixins are constructs that let you define blocks of CSS code that can be used repeatedly in various parts of your project. They let you combine several properties and apply them to different elements, which reduces the amount of duplicated code.\\nAn example of a mixin:\\n\\nIn this example the `button-styles` mixin is used to define a button\'s main styles. It can be included in different classes, letting you keep things consistent.\\nFunctions\\nFunctions in CSS preprocessors let you perform calculations and return values. This can be useful for creating dynamic styles or for managing values such as colours, sizes and spacing.\\nAn example of a function:\\n\\nIn this example the `calculate-spacing` function takes a parameter and returns the value multiplied by 10 pixels. This lets you manage the spacing and sizes in a project dynamically.\\nUsing functions and mixins in CSS preprocessors simplifies work with styles considerably, improves their structure and makes code more manageable. This matters especially on large projects, where reuse and the reduction of duplicated code become critically important.","@mixin button-styles {\\n  padding: 10px 20px;\\n  border-radius: 5px;\\n  color: white;\\n  background-color: blue;\\n}\\n.btn-primary {\\n  @include button-styles;\\n}\\n.btn-secondary {\\n  @include button-styles;\\n  background-color: green;\\n}\\n\\n@function calculate-spacing($factor) {\\n  @return $factor * 10px;\\n}\\n.element {\\n  margin: calculate-spacing(2); // It will return 20px\\n}"],[362,"CSS works by going through several stages, from parsing to rendering. When the browser loads an HTML document, it breaks the CSS into rules and applies them to the elements. A display model is then created, in which the browser computes how every element will look on the screen. Finally the browser paints the elements on the page. Applying CSS effectively and optimizing it can improve a site\'s performance considerably.","When the browser loads a web page, it goes through several stages of processing in order to display the CSS styles correctly. Let us look at this process in more detail:\\n\\n- Parsing the CSS\\nWhen the browser receives an HTML document, it loads the CSS files at the same time. The browser breaks the CSS into rules, identifying the selectors and the declarations belonging to them. This process involves:\\n\\n- Syntactic analysis: the browser checks whether the CSS document conforms to the standards. If there are errors, they will be reported, but the browser will still try to process the valid parts.\\n\\nCreating the style tree: at this stage the browser creates a style tree, which links the CSS rules to the corresponding HTML elements.\\n\\n- Building the render tree\\n\\nAfter parsing the CSS and the HTML, the browser creates the render tree, which represents the page\'s visual structure. This process involves:\\n\\n- Combining the DOM and the CSSOM: the render tree consists of DOM (Document Object Model) nodes but includes only the visible elements that have styles. Invisible elements, such as those with display: none, are ignored.\\n\\n- Computing the dimensions: the browser computes the dimensions and positioning of every element on the page. This matters in order to know how each element will be displayed on the screen.\\n\\n- Rendering\\n\\nAt the rendering stage the browser draws the page on the screen:\\n\\n- The cascade and inheritance: CSS works on the basis of cascading and inheritance. This means styles can be inherited from parent elements, and if several rules apply to the same element the browser decides which to use based on specificity and order.\\n\\n- Painting: the browser paints the elements on the screen based on the render tree it has built. Every element receives the corresponding style, and the browser outputs them in the right order.\\n\\n- Updates and layout\\n\\nIf the user interacts with the elements on a page, the browser may update the display, which can require recomputation:\\n\\n- Recalculating the styles: changes to styles can affect the display, and the browser recomputes the dimensions and positions of the elements.\\n\\n- Repainting: if the styles change but the dimensions stay the same, the browser performs only a repaint, without recomputing the layout — which is considerably cheaper. If the geometry changes, a reflow follows, and only then a repaint.",null],[363,"CSS lets you separate a web page\'s content from its styling, which makes code more manageable and cleaner. It supports responsive design, letting pages look good on various devices. CSS also improves performance by reducing the size of the HTML document, and it lets styles be reused, which makes code easier to maintain. In addition, CSS lets you create animations and visual effects, improving the user interface.","CSS has a great many advantages that make it an inseparable part of web development. Let us look at some of them:\\n\\n- Separating content from presentation\\n\\nCSS lets you separate HTML content from its visual styling. This means developers can change a page\'s style without touching the content\'s structure. Such a separation makes code better organized and simpler to maintain.\\n\\n- Adaptive, responsive design\\n\\nWith CSS, developers can create responsive web pages that adapt automatically to different screen sizes. Using media queries, you can change the styles according to the device, which ensures an optimal display on mobiles, tablets and desktop computers.\\n\\n- Reusing styles\\n\\nCSS lets you reuse styles through classes and identifiers. This means the same style can be applied to a great many elements, which reduces the amount of duplicated code and makes it easier to maintain.\\n\\n- Better performance\\n\\nSeparate CSS files can be cached by the browser, which reduces the loading time of pages on later visits. In addition, using CSS for styling reduces the volume of HTML code, which also improves performance.\\n\\n- Visual effects and animations\\n\\nCSS provides tools for creating animations and transitions, letting developers add interactive elements and improve the user interface without any need for JavaScript.",null],[364,"The latest version of CSS is CSS3, which brought a great many new capabilities, such as new selectors, media queries, animations, gradients and background images. What is special about CSS3 is that it introduces a modular structure, which lets developers use only the modules they need and adopt new features gradually. This makes CSS a more flexible and powerful tool for web development.","CSS3 is the latest version of the CSS specification and was developed in order to extend the functionality and improve the capabilities of web design. The main characteristics and innovations of CSS3 include:\\n\\n- The modular structure\\n\\nCSS3 is presented as a set of many modules, each of which is responsible for particular features. This lets developers use only the modules they need and update them independently of one another. Examples of modules include Flexbox, Grid, Transitions and Animations.\\n\\n- New selectors and pseudo-classes\\n\\nCSS3 adds new selectors and pseudo-classes that make selecting the elements on a page easier. The pseudo-class :nth-child(), for example, lets you select elements based on their position within their parent element.\\n\\n- Animations and transitions\\n\\nCSS3 provides the ability to create animations and smooth transitions, which lets developers add interactive, dynamic elements to pages without using JavaScript.\\n\\n- Borders, shadows and gradients\\n\\nCSS3 lets you add gradients, shadows and rounded edges to elements. This improves the visual styling of pages and lets you create more modern designs.\\n\\n- Flexbox and Grid\\n\\nCSS3 introduces new layout models such as Flexbox and Grid, which considerably simplify the creation of responsive layouts. These technologies let developers arrange elements in rows and columns easily, controlling their placement and size.",null],[365,"Flexbox (Flexible Box Layout) gives developers the ability to control the placement and alignment of the elements inside a container easily. The main Flexbox properties include:\\n\\n- `display: flex`\\n\\n- `flex-direction`\\n\\n- `justify-content`\\n\\n- `align-items`\\n\\n- `flex-wrap`\\n\\nThese properties let you control the direction, the alignment and the behaviour of elements, making them more responsive on different screens.","Flexbox, or Flexible Box Layout, is a layout method in CSS intended for creating more flexible, responsive layouts. Flexbox simplifies the process of aligning elements and distributing space between them inside a container. Let us look at Flexbox\'s main properties:\\n\\n- `display: flex`\\nThis property makes an element a flex container, and its child elements become flex items.\\n\\n- `flex-direction`\\nThis property determines the direction in which the flex items will be placed. The possible values are: row (the default), row-reverse, column, column-reverse.\\n\\n- `justify-content`\\nThe property controls the alignment of the flex items along the main axis (in the direction of flex-direction). The possible values are: flex-start, flex-end, center, space-between, space-around.\\n\\n- `align-items`\\nThis property controls the alignment of the flex items along the cross axis (perpendicular to the main axis). The possible values are: flex-start, flex-end, center, baseline, stretch.\\n\\n- `flex-wrap`\\nThe property determines whether the flex items should wrap onto a new line if they do not fit in the container. The values are: nowrap (the default), wrap, wrap-reverse.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe uses of Flexbox\\nFlexbox is often used to create responsive layouts such as navigation bars, card layouts and image galleries. It lets you control space and the alignment of elements easily, which makes the development process faster and more convenient.",".container {\\n  display: flex;\\n  flex-direction: row; /* The items are placed horizontally */\\n  justify-content: center; /* The items are aligned to the centre */\\n  align-items: stretch; /* The items stretch to the container\'s height */\\n  flex-wrap: wrap; /* The items will wrap onto the next line */ \\n}"],[366,"The cascade in CSS determines how styles are applied when several rules apply to the same element. The rules of the cascade are based on the specificity of the selectors and the order in which the styles are defined. Inheritance is the mechanism by which child elements receive styles from their parents.\\nIf a parent element has a text colour set, for example, the child elements will inherit that colour unless styles of their own have been set for them.","The cascade and inheritance are the two main principles governing how CSS applies styles to the elements on a web page.\\nThe cascade\\nThe cascade in CSS describes how browsers handle several conflicting rules. When two or more rules apply to the same element, the browser uses the following criteria to determine which rule to apply:\\n\\n- The specificity of the selectors: more specific selectors take priority. A rule with an ID (#header), for example, is more specific than a rule with a class (.header).\\n\\n- The order of definition: if two rules have the same specificity, the rule defined later in the code takes priority.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nIn this case the text in the element with the ID header will be red, since the rule with the ID has the higher specificity.\\nInheritance\\nInheritance is the mechanism that lets child elements inherit styles from their parents. This is convenient, since developers can set styles on a parent element and have them applied to its children automatically, which reduces the amount of code.\\nAn example:\\n\\nIn this example every child element inside .container will use the Arial font unless one of its own has been set for it.\\nExceptions to inheritance\\nNot every property is inherited. The properties margin, padding and border, for example, are not inherited. So if you want to set these styles on child elements, you have to do it explicitly.\\nThe use of the cascade and inheritance\\nUnderstanding the cascade and inheritance lets developers manage styles more effectively and avoid conflicts.",".header {\\n  color: blue; /* 1 */\\n}\\n#header {\\n  color: red; /* 2 */\\n}\\n\\n.container {\\n  font-family: Arial, sans-serif; /* The font is set on the container */\\n}\\n.child {\\n  /* It inherits the font from the container */\\n}"],[367,"CSS variables are user-defined custom properties that can be reused in various places in your styles. They are declared with the `--` prefix and can be changed according to the context — inside media queries, for example. This simplifies the management of styles and lets you adapt a design more flexibly.","CSS variables (or custom properties) are values that can be set and reused in your styles. They are declared with the syntax `--variable-name: value;` and can be used in style rules through the `var(--variable-name)` function.\\nVariables simplify the process of changing styles, since they let you set values centrally — for colours, sizes or other properties, for example. In addition they are inherited and can change according to conditions (such as media queries or states), which makes them useful for creating responsive designs and themes.",null],[368,"CSS Modules are a method of organizing CSS that generates unique class names automatically, in order to avoid name conflicts and make styles local to a component. This helps keep a codebase clean and reduces the chance of unexpected changes to styles.","CSS Modules are an approach to styling in which styles are localized to a particular component.\\nDuring compilation the classes and identifiers are given unique names, which prevents the name conflicts and cascade problems characteristic of global styles.\\nThis approach lets you divide styles into modules and keep them better structured and more readable. CSS Modules are often used in React projects to organize styles by component, which helps make a project more scalable and more resilient to change.",null],[369,"You should avoid complex CSS selectors, such as deeply nested structures or the use of the universal selector (`*`). It is also not recommended to apply styles directly to a large number of elements, or to use expensive animations without hardware acceleration.","Rendering performance in CSS can suffer because of complex selectors, which force the browser to walk over a great many elements. Examples of such selectors include the universal selector or long chains of nesting.\\nYou should also avoid frequent changes to properties that affect the layout flow, such as `width`, `height` and `margin`, since that causes every style to be recomputed. It is better to use hardware-accelerated properties such as `transform` and `opacity` for animations.\\nOptimizing styles, especially on large projects, improves rendering performance considerably.",null],[370,"CSS-in-JS libraries let you write styles right in your JavaScript code. In `styled-components` the styles are created as components, which makes it easier to change styles dynamically based on state and to use themes.","CSS-in-JS libraries such as `styled-components` provide the ability to write a component\'s styles in JavaScript, which lets you create dynamic, styled components. An example of using `styled-components` in React involves importing the library and creating a component with styles:\\n\\nThis simplifies the creation and management of dynamic styles, since the styles can be changed according to the component\'s state or the props it was given. The approach also supports themes, which makes it useful for building responsive, scalable interfaces.","import styled from \'styled-components\';\\nconst Button = styled.button`\\n  background: blue;\\n  color: white;\\n  padding: 10px 20px;\\n  border-radius: 5px;\\n  &:hover {\\n      background: darkblue;\\n  }\\n`;\\nexport default Button;"],[371,"CSS Houdini is a set of APIs that lets developers interact with the browser\'s rendering engine and create CSS effects and properties of their own. Houdini gives you the ability to write JavaScript in order to extend the functionality of CSS, adding new capabilities for styling.","CSS Houdini is a technology that provides access to the browser\'s internal processes, letting you create and apply styles and effects of your own at the style-compilation stage.\\nHoudini includes several APIs, such as the Paint API (creating your own drawings and backgrounds), the Layout API (defining your own layout schemes) and the Typed OM (access to the style object model).\\nWith the Paint API, for example, you can create a background generated dynamically from data, while the Layout API lets you define unique layout behaviour. This makes styles more powerful and more configurable.",null],[372,"To ensure cross-browser font compatibility you can use several formats, such as WOFF, WOFF2 and TTF. The WOFF2 format compresses better, while TTF is compatible with older browsers. Fonts are linked in through the `@font-face` directive, listing all the necessary formats.","For cross-browser font loading the `@font-face` directive is normally used, listing the paths to the different font formats. Here is an example of how that can be implemented:\\n\\nWith this approach the browser will pick the most suitable format, which gives the best performance. The WOFF2 format is considered preferable for modern browsers thanks to its efficiency.","@font-face {\\n  font-family: \'MyFont\';\\n  src: url(\'/fonts/myfont.woff2\') format(\'woff2\'),\\n    url(\'/fonts/myfont.woff\') format(\'woff\'),\\n    url(\'/fonts/myfont.ttf\') format(\'truetype\');\\n  font-weight: normal;\\n  font-style: normal;\\n}"],[373,"Responsive typography involves the use of units such as `em`, `rem` and `%`, and of modern functions such as `clamp()`. These methods help make text flexible and able to adjust to the size of the screen.","There are various approaches to creating responsive typography: the use of relative units (`em`, `rem`), approaches based on media queries, or dynamic functions (`clamp()`, `calc()`). With the `clamp()` function, for example, you can set sizes that adapt to the width of the screen:\\n\\nThis lets you set a minimum and a maximum value, with the size between them chosen automatically according to the width of the window.","font-size: clamp(1rem, 2vw + 1rem, 2rem);"],[374,"The `calc()`, `var()` and `clamp()` functions let you perform calculations inside CSS.\\n\\n- `calc()` is used for arithmetic operations\\n\\n- `var()` — for using CSS variables\\n\\n- `clamp()` helps you set values with a minimum and a maximum bound","CSS functions let you perform dynamic calculations and set flexible styles.\\n`calc()` is useful for mixing units, for example:\\n`width: calc(100% - 2rem);`\\n`var()` is used for working with CSS variables, which lets you override values in different places:\\n\\n`clamp()` lets you set a value that will vary within given bounds, for example:\\n`font-size: clamp(1rem, 2vw + 1rem, 2.5rem);`\\nThis is useful for building responsive interfaces.","--main-color: #3498db; \\nbackground-color: var(--main-color);"],[375,"The `@supports` directive lets you check whether the browser supports a specific CSS property and apply styles only if the check succeeds. This is useful for making use of new capabilities while retaining compatibility.","The `@supports` directive helps you apply styles conditionally, depending on the browser\'s support for particular CSS functions or properties. An example of its use:\\n\\nIf the browser supports `display: grid`, the corresponding styles will be applied. This helps you use new technologies while retaining backwards compatibility.","@supports (display: grid) {\\n  .container {\\n    display: grid;\\n  }\\n}"],[376,"Modern CSS colour functions, such as `rgba()`, `hsla()`, `hwb()` and `lab()`, let you describe colours using different models, which gives developers more flexibility. `rgba()`, for example, adds transparency to a colour, `hsla()` uses hue, saturation and lightness, and `lab()` describes colours in a way that is more natural to perception.","Modern CSS colour functions give developers various ways of defining a colour, based on different models. Here are some of them:\\n\\n- rgba() (Red, Green, Blue, Alpha):\\nThis function sets a colour through its red, green and blue components plus an alpha-transparency parameter. An example:\\n\\nThe alpha value indicates the degree of transparency, where 1 is opaque and 0 is fully transparent.\\n\\n- hsla() (Hue, Saturation, Lightness, Alpha):\\nThe `hsla()` model uses hue (from 0 to 360 degrees), saturation and lightness (as percentages), plus transparency. An example:\\n\\n- hwb() (Hue, Whiteness, Blackness):\\nThe `hwb()` function is similar to `hsl()`, but instead of saturation it uses the percentage of white and of black. An example:\\n\\n- lab():\\nThe LAB colour model describes a colour on the basis of human perception, which makes it more convenient to work with colours. It consists of three values: L (lightness), A and B (the colour axes). An example:\\n\\nThese functions let developers create flexible, responsive colour schemes using different ways of describing a colour. `rgba()` and `hsla()`, for example, are often used for semi-transparent overlays and gradients, while `lab()` is suited to fine-tuning colour transitions close to the way the human eye perceives them.","color: rgba(255, 0, 0, 0.5); /* Semi-transparent red */\\n\\ncolor: hsla(120, 100%, 50%, 0.3); /* Semi-transparent saturated green */\\n\\ncolor: hwb(240, 0%, 0%); /* Pure blue */\\n\\ncolor: lab(75% 20 -30); /* A colour with 75% lightness */"],[377,"The `:is()` pseudo-class lets you shorten the notation for complex selectors by combining several selectors in a single expression. It makes code lighter and more readable, applying styles to the elements that match any of the selectors listed in `:is()`.","The `:is()` pseudo-class is used to shorten the notation for complex CSS selectors and to simplify the styling of elements. It lets you combine several selectors in one expression and applies styles to any element that matches one of the selectors listed inside `:is()`. For example:\\n\\nThis code changes the style of the text inserted with the `::before` and `::after` pseudo-elements for every element with the class `.cat-list`, making it uppercase. Unlike ordinary pseudo-classes, `:is()` also helps optimize CSS, since it takes into account the specificity of only the selector that sits inside `:is()`.",".cat-list:is(::before, ::after) {\\n  text-transform: uppercase;\\n}"],[378,"To protect yourself against CSS injection you should avoid generating CSS dynamically from user data. Using proven libraries and validating input strictly will help minimize the risk of such attacks.","CSS injection is a type of attack in which an attacker can insert malicious code into a page\'s styles in order to change the appearance or the behaviour of the site. The main means of protection include:\\n\\n- Do not insert user data into CSS without validation. Never generate styles from data received from users without strict validation.\\n\\n- Use safe libraries and tools. If you need to apply styles dynamically, it is recommended that you use proven CSS-in-JS solutions such as `styled-components`, which escape potentially dangerous data automatically.\\n\\n- Restrict the ability to load external styles. You should avoid using `@import`, for example, and should not load styles from untrusted sources.",null],[379,"The use of external styles and fonts can slow a page\'s loading down, and can also represent a security threat if the resources are loaded from untrusted sources. Keeping control over the loading and caching of such resources helps minimize the risks.","The main risks associated with the use of external styles and fonts include:\\n\\n- Slower page loading. Loading external resources increases a page\'s response time, since it requires additional HTTP requests. This is especially critical on mobile devices and slow connections.\\n\\n- Security problems. External styles or fonts can be modified on the server side, which creates potential threats. Attackers can inject malicious code into styles or fonts — through a content-substitution attack, for example.\\n\\n- Cross-browser problems. Some fonts and styles may not work identically in every browser, which will break the visual design.\\n\\nTo minimize the risks it is recommended that you load fonts locally or use trusted sources that support a safe-loading policy, and that you also provide for caching and for optimizing the loading of resources.",null],[380,"Critical CSS is the set of styles needed for a page\'s initial display, which are inlined into the HTML in order to avoid a loading delay. The remaining styles are loaded asynchronously. Lazy-loading styles involves using JavaScript to link in or load CSS conditionally, which lets you load styles as they are needed.","Critical CSS comprises the styles needed to display the content that sits \\"above the fold\\", and is inlined directly into the HTML document in order to speed the first paint up.\\nAfter that the remaining styles can be loaded asynchronously, using the `rel=\\"preload\\"` attribute or `media=\\"print\\"` with a subsequent change to `media=\\"all\\"`, so as to defer their application until the page has loaded. This prevents the main page\'s rendering from being blocked, and once loading is complete it lets you make the styles active for every device.\\nLazy-loading styles lets you link CSS files in as they are needed: with JavaScript, for example, to load styles when the user scrolls to a particular section, or using the `media` attribute to link styles in when a given condition is met.\\nYou can also use bundlers and tools such as `Webpack` and `critical`, which pick out the critical styles automatically and simplify the optimization process.\\nAn example of using lazy loading:\\n\\nIn this example the styles are loaded asynchronously, since `media=\\"print\\"` initially prevents them from being applied to the screen display. Once they have loaded the attribute is changed to `media=\\"all\\"`, and the styles begin to apply to every type of device.","<link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"styles.css\\" media=\\"print\\" onload=\\"this.media=\'all\'\\">"],[381,"SVG has a number of advantages over raster graphics, such as scaling without any loss of quality, a small file size and the ability to apply styles through CSS. SVG can, however, be awkward for representing photographs and can have performance problems when displaying complex or highly detailed images.","SVG (Scalable Vector Graphics) is a vector image format that describes graphics by means of XML code. SVG\'s main advantages include:\\n\\n- Scalability: SVG retains a high image quality at any scale.\\n\\n- A small file size: for simple graphical elements an SVG\'s size can be considerably smaller than that of a raster image.\\n\\n- Editability and style: SVG can be styled with CSS and JavaScript, which simplifies animation and interaction.\\n\\n- SEO friendliness: the text inside an SVG is available for search and indexing.\\n\\nThe disadvantages include the difficulty of representing photographs and a potential drop in performance when using complex SVGs made up of a great many objects.",null],[382,"SVG can be embedded directly in HTML code or used as a background image in CSS. Inline SVG supports styling through ordinary CSS properties such as `fill` and `stroke`, which makes it easy to change its colour and transparency.","SVG can be used in CSS in several ways:\\n\\n- As a background image: added with `background-image` in CSS. For example:\\n\\n- Embedded in HTML: inserted directly into the HTML and styled through CSS. For example:\\n\\n- Here the `.icon` class can control the SVG\'s style through CSS:\\n\\nThe use of CSS properties: the properties `fill`, `stroke` and `opacity`, as well as transforms, can be applied to the elements inside an SVG, controlling their appearance and their behaviour.",".icon {\\n  background-image: url(\'icon.svg\');\\n}\\n\\n<svg class=\\"icon\\" xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\">\\n  <circle cx=\\"50\\" cy=\\"50\\" r=\\"40\\" fill=\\"red\\" />\\n</svg>\\n\\n.icon circle {\\n  fill: blue;\\n}"],[383,"CSS can be used to improve accessibility by increasing the contrast of text, using visible focus styles for interactive elements, and ensuring a comfortable font size and good control over space. You can also style elements with screen-reader support in mind and hide elements without getting in the way of navigation.","To improve the accessibility of web pages with CSS you can use the following approaches:\\n\\n- Contrast: ensuring a high contrast between the text and the background improves readability for users with impaired vision. To check contrast you can use tools such as WebAIM.\\n\\n- Focusable elements: it is important that focus be visible when the user interacts with elements using the keyboard. This can be done by styling the `:focus` pseudo-class, for example:\\n\\n- Font size and space: the use of relative units such as `em` and `rem` lets you adapt font sizes and spacing according to the user\'s settings.\\n\\n- Hiding content: CSS lets you hide elements with `visibility: hidden` or `display: none`, while the `aria-hidden` attribute can be used to control the visibility of elements for screen readers.","button:focus {\\n  outline: 2px solid #4a90e2;\\n}"],[384,"Atomic CSS and Utility-First CSS are approaches to writing styles in which every rule represents a single CSS property or utility. In Utility-First CSS, for example, classes are used that define specific styles such as margins or colours, which lets you build interfaces quickly.","Atomic CSS is an approach in which every CSS rule represents one property — the class `.mt-4` for setting a top margin, for example. This lets you use small, flexible classes to build complex interfaces.\\nUtility-First CSS (Tailwind CSS, for example) extends the idea of Atomic CSS by providing ready-made utilities for most CSS properties. For example:\\n\\nHere `bg-blue-500`, `text-white` and `p-4` are utility classes that set the background, the text colour and the padding respectively.\\nThese approaches help you build interfaces quickly and avoid writing new CSS rules, but they can lead to bloated HTML files if they are not used carefully.","<div class=\\"bg-blue-500 text-white p-4\\">\\n  This is an example of the Utility-First style\\n</div>"],[385,"CSS Grid lets you create flexible layouts with properties such as `grid-template-columns`, `grid-template-rows` and `grid-template-areas`. These properties help you control the placement of elements and adapt a layout for different screens using media queries.","CSS Grid provides powerful capabilities for creating grids. The `grid-template-areas` property lets you define named areas that can be used to place elements. For example:\\n\\nThis code creates a grid in which `header` takes up the whole width, `sidebar` and `content` sit side by side, and `footer` is at the bottom.\\nTo adapt to different screens you can use media queries:\\n\\nThe layout will therefore rearrange itself according to the size of the screen, making the page responsive.",".container {\\n  display: grid;\\n  grid-template-areas: \\n    \\"header header\\"\\n    \\"sidebar content\\"\\n    \\"footer footer\\";\\n  grid-template-columns: 1fr 2fr;\\n  grid-template-rows: auto;\\n}\\n.header {\\n  grid-area: header;\\n}\\n.sidebar {\\n  grid-area: sidebar;\\n}\\n.content {\\n  grid-area: content;\\n}\\n.footer {\\n  grid-area: footer;\\n}\\n\\n@media (max-width: 768px) {\\n  .container {\\n    grid-template-areas: \\n      \\"header\\"\\n      \\"content\\"\\n      \\"sidebar\\"\\n      \\"footer\\";\\n    grid-template-columns: 1fr;\\n  }\\n}"],[386,"CSS can be used to support multilingual websites and localization by applying styles that depend on the direction of the text (LTR or RTL) or on the language. With attribute selectors on `[lang]` you can adapt the appearance of text for different languages, and also control the placement and alignment of elements according to the language settings.","Support for multiple languages and for localization in CSS can be implemented in several ways:\\n\\n- Support for text directions (LTR and RTL): in different languages text is read left to right (LTR) or right to left (RTL). To support RTL you can use the `direction` property and adapt your styles so that everything displays correctly:\\n\\nThis property changes the direction of the text and the placement of elements for languages such as Arabic or Hebrew. For flexibility you can use media queries or JavaScript in order to change `direction` dynamically according to the current localization.\\n\\n- The use of attribute selectors on `[lang]`: `[lang]` selectors let you apply styles to particular languages. For specific styles for Japanese text, for example:\\n\\nThis helps you adapt fonts and styles to the language in question, which gives better readability.\\n\\n- Changing styles according to the localization: some languages need more room for their text, so it is important to allow for changes in font sizes, element widths and padding when localizing. For example:\\n\\nThis will allow for the differences in word length between languages.\\n\\n- Text alignment: you can change the alignment of text according to the language:\\n\\nThis will make text display correctly in languages with different writing directions.\\n\\n- The use of the `:lang()` pseudo-class: the `:lang()` pseudo-class lets you apply styles according to the language:\\n\\nThis will help you create unique styles for different languages.","body {\\n  direction: rtl;\\n}\\n\\n[lang=\\"ja\\"] {\\n  font-family: \\"Noto Sans JP\\", sans-serif;\\n}\\n\\n[lang=\\"fr\\"] .button {\\n  width: 150px;\\n}\\n[lang=\\"en\\"] .button {\\n  width: 120px;\\n}\\n\\n[lang=\\"ar\\"] {\\n  text-align: right;\\n}\\n[lang=\\"en\\"] {\\n  text-align: left;\\n}\\n\\np:lang(ru) {\\n  font-style: italic;\\n}"],[387,"The `<span>` element will become a block: it will take up the whole width of its parent, will start on a new line and will be able to have a width/height, in contrast to inline behaviour.","By default `<span>` is an inline element (`display: inline`). If you change it to `display: block`:\\n\\n- Width: it stretches to the full available width of its parent (like a `<div>`).\\n\\n- Flow: it starts on a new line, even if it is inside text.\\n\\n- Dimensions: you can set `width/height` and `margin/padding` (which work differently for `inline`).\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nSuch a `<span>` will behave like a `<div>`. This is useful if you need to change the document flow without replacing the tag in the HTML.\\nFor semantic markup, however, it is better to use a `<div>` if you need a block.","<span style=\\"display: block; background: lightblue; width: 200px;\\">\\n  This is now a block element.\\n</span>"],[388,"You can use `position: fixed` plus JavaScript to calculate the element\'s position dynamically as the page scrolls. You can also use an `IntersectionObserver` to track the element\'s position and switch its styles.","- Using `position: fixed` + JavaScript\\n\\nIf `sticky` is not available, you can imitate its behaviour:\\n\\n- As the page scrolls, calculate when the element should \\"stick\\".\\n\\n- Switch `position: relative` → `position: fixed` when it reaches the boundary.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\n- Using an `IntersectionObserver`\\n\\nA more modern way is to track when the element reaches the boundary:\\n\\n- An alternative with `transform: translateY`\\n\\nIf you need smooth behaviour, you can animate the element\'s position:","const stickyElement = document.querySelector(\'.sticky\');\\nconst offset = stickyElement.offsetTop;\\nwindow.addEventListener(\'scroll\', () => {\\n  if (window.scrollY >= offset) {\\n    stickyElement.style.position = \'fixed\';\\n    stickyElement.style.top = \'0\';\\n  } else {\\n    stickyElement.style.position = \'relative\';\\n  }\\n});\\n\\nconst observer = new IntersectionObserver(\\n    (entries) => {\\n        entries.forEach((entry) => {\\n            if (entry.isIntersecting) {\\n              entry.target.style.position = \'fixed\';\\n              entry.target.style.top = \'0\';\\n            } else {\\n              entry.target.style.position = \'relative\';\\n            }\\n        });\\n    },\\n    { threshold: 1 }\\n);\\n\\nobserver.observe(document.querySelector(\'.sticky\'));\\n\\n.sticky {\\n  position: relative;\\n  transition: transform 0.2s;\\n}\\n.sticky.fixed {\\n  transform: translateY(var(--scroll-offset));\\n}\\n\\nwindow.addEventListener(\'scroll\', () => {\\n  const scrollY = window.scrollY;\\n  stickyElement.classList.toggle(\'fixed\', scrollY > offset);\\n});"],[389,"An element\'s final width is affected by:\\n\\n- `width` — sets the base width of the content.\\n\\n- `padding` — the inner spacing (added to `width`).\\n\\n- `border` — the thickness of the border (which also increases the total width).\\n\\nIf `box-sizing: border-box` is set, `width` includes `padding` and `border`.","1. The base width (`width`)\\nDetermines the width of the element\'s content.\\n\\n2. The inner spacing (`padding`)\\nIs added to `width`, increasing the total size.\\n\\n3. The border (`border`)\\nAlso increases the total width.\\n\\nHow does `box-sizing` change the calculations?\\nIf you set `box-sizing: border-box`, `width` will include `padding` and `border`:\\n\\nWhat else can affect the width?\\n\\n- `margin` — the outer spacing (it is not part of the total width, but it does take up room on the page).\\n\\n- `min-width`/`max-width` — they constrain `width`.\\n\\nIn summary:\\n\\n- Without `border-box`: `width` + `padding` + `border`.\\n\\n- With `border-box`: `width` includes everything.","div {\\n  width: 200px; /* The width of the content */\\n}\\n\\ndiv {\\n  width: 200px;\\n  padding: 20px; /* +40px to the total width (20px on the left and on the right) */\\n}\\n/* Final width: 200px (content) + 40px (padding) = 240px */\\n\\ndiv {\\n  width: 200px;\\n  padding: 20px;\\n  border: 5px solid black; /* +10px to the width (5px on the left and on the right) */\\n}\\n/* Final width: 200 + 40 + 10 = 250px */\\n\\ndiv {\\n  box-sizing: border-box;\\n  width: 200px; /* This is now the total width */\\n  padding: 20px; /* Included in the 200px */\\n  border: 5px solid black; /* Also included in the 200px */\\n  /* The width of the content: 200 - 40 (padding) - 10 (border) = 150px */\\n}"],[390,"The main values are:\\n\\n- `block` — the element takes up the whole width and starts on a new line.\\n\\n- `inline` — the element takes up only as much width as it needs and does not wrap onto a new line.\\n\\n- `inline-block` — like `inline`, but with the ability to set a width/height.\\n\\n- `none` — the element is hidden and takes up no room.","1. `block`:\\n\\n- Examples: `<div>`, `<p>`, `<h1>`.\\n\\n- You can set `width`, `height`, `margin` and `padding`.\\n\\n2. `inline`:\\n\\n- Examples: `<span>`, `<a>`, `<strong>`.\\n\\n- You cannot set `width`, `height` or `margin-top/bottom`.\\n\\n3. `inline-block`:\\n\\n- Combines the characteristics of `inline` and `block`.\\n\\n- An example: buttons (`<button>` is `inline-block` by default).\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nConclusion:\\nThe choice of `display` depends on the task: laying out blocks, text or hybrid elements.",".block {\\n  display: block;\\n  width: 200px;\\n  background: red;\\n}\\n\\n.inline {\\n  display: inline;\\n  /* width and height will have no effect */\\n}\\n\\n.inline-block {\\n  display: inline-block;\\n  width: 100px;\\n  height: 50px;\\n}"],[391,"- `flex-direction` sets the direction of the main axis (`row` — horizontal, `column` — vertical).\\n\\n- `justify-content` aligns the items along the main axis.\\n\\n- `align-items` aligns them along the cross axis.","1. The axes in Flexbox:\\n\\n- The main axis: the direction set by `flex-direction` (`row` by default).\\n\\n- The cross axis: perpendicular to the main one.\\n\\nExamples:\\nCase 1: `flex-direction: row`\\n\\n- The main axis → horizontal.\\n\\n- `justify-content: center` → centres horizontally.\\n\\n- `align-items: center` → centres vertically.\\n\\nCase 2: `flex-direction: column`\\n\\n- The main axis → vertical.\\n\\n- `justify-content: center` → centres vertically.\\n\\n- `align-items: center` → centres horizontally.\\n\\nA visualization:\\n\\nConclusion:\\nThe direction set by `flex-direction` changes the logic by which `justify-content` and `align-items` work.","flex-direction: row          flex-direction: column\\n[ → → → → ] (main)          [ ↑ (main)\\n[ ↓ ] (cross)                [ → ] (cross)"],[392,"- By default (`content-box`): `width` sets only the width of the content, and `padding` and `border` are added to it.\\n\\n- `border-box`: `width` includes `padding` and `border`, which simplifies layout.","A comparison:\\n\\nWhy use `border-box`?\\n\\n- Predictability: the `width` you set is the final width.\\n\\n- It simplifies responsive layout.\\n\\nConclusion:\\n`border-box` is the standard in modern layout. It is applied globally:","/* content-box (the default) */\\n.div1 {\\n  width: 200px;\\n  padding: 20px;\\n  border: 5px solid;\\n  /* The actual width: 200 + 40 + 10 = 250px */\\n}\\n\\n/* border-box */\\n.div2 {\\n  box-sizing: border-box;\\n  width: 200px;\\n  padding: 20px;\\n  border: 5px solid;\\n  /* The actual width: 200px (including padding and border) */\\n}\\n\\n* { box-sizing: border-box; }"],[393,"- The element behaves like `relative` until its parent has scrolled to the specified boundary (`top`, `left` and so on), and then becomes `fixed`.\\n\\n- It \\"unsticks\\" when the parent container has scrolled all the way past.","An example:\\n\\nThe conditions for it to work:\\n\\n- The parent must have a scrolling area (`overflow: auto`, for example).\\n\\n- `sticky` works only inside the parent — when the scroll goes beyond the parent\'s bounds the element \\"unsticks\\".\\n\\nConclusion:\\n`sticky` is ideal for table headers or menus that should stay visible as the page scrolls.",".sticky-element {\\n  position: sticky;\\n  top: 20px; /* It sticks when the distance to the top of the viewport is ≤ 20px */\\n}"],[394,"- `relative`: the neighbouring elements stay where they are, as if there had been no shift.\\n\\n- `absolute`: the element is removed from the flow, and its neighbours take its place.","1. `relative`:\\n\\n- The element is shifted, but its original place remains occupied.\\n\\n- An example:\\n\\n2. `absolute`:\\n\\n- The element is \\"torn out\\" of the flow.\\n\\n- An example:\\n\\nConclusion:\\n`relative` preserves the flow; `absolute` breaks it.","<div style=\\"position: relative; top: 20px;\\">Shifted</div>\\n<div>The neighbour stayed where it was</div>\\n\\n<div style=\\"position: absolute; top: 0;\\">Absolute</div>\\n<div>The neighbour moved up into its place</div>"],[395,"- Two-dimensional layouts: grids with rows and columns.\\n\\n- Precise placement: items can be placed in any cell.\\n\\n- Control over gaps: managing the spacing (`gap`) between rows/columns.","Examples of tasks for Grid:\\n\\n- Complex grids:\\n\\n- Overlapping items:\\n\\n- Asymmetric layouts:\\n\\nWhere Flexbox is better:\\n\\n- One-dimensional containers (navigation, for example).\\n\\n- Aligning items inside a container.\\n\\nConclusion:\\nGrid is for complex grids; Flexbox is for linear ones.",".container {\\n  display: grid;\\n  grid-template-columns: 1fr 2fr 1fr;\\n  grid-template-rows: 100px auto;\\n}\\n\\n.item1 { grid-area: 1 / 1 / 2 / 3; }\\n.item2 { grid-area: 1 / 2 / 2 / 4; }\\n\\ngrid-template-areas:\\n  \\"header header header\\"\\n  \\"sidebar main main\\";"],[396,"Pseudo-elements (`::before`, `::after`) do not create real DOM nodes; the browser renders them at the CSS level. This reduces the load on the DOM tree and speeds rendering up.","The problem with DOM nodes:\\n\\n- Each of the 10k elements is added to the DOM tree, which requires:\\n\\n- Allocating memory.\\n\\n- Recomputing the styles and the layout (reflow/repaint).\\n\\n- An example:\\n\\nThe advantages of pseudo-elements:\\n\\n- No load on the DOM:\\n\\n- Pseudo-elements exist only in the CSSOM (the CSS Object Model).\\n\\n- The browser optimizes the way they are painted.\\n\\n- An example of their use:\\n\\nEven for 10k elements with the class `.item` — just one CSS rule instead of 10k nodes.\\n\\nConclusion:\\nFor decorative elements (icons, markers) use `::before`/`::after` instead of JavaScript/the DOM.","for (let i = 0; i < 10000; i++) {\\n  const div = document.createElement(\'div\');\\n  div.textContent = \'Element \' + i;\\n  document.body.appendChild(div); // 10k new nodes!\\n}\\n\\n.item::before {\\n  content: \\"•\\";\\n  margin-right: 5px;\\n}"],[397,"Flexbox manages axes through `flex-direction` (the main axis) and `align-items`/`justify-content` (alignment). It is better than `float`, because:\\n\\n- It lets you align elements easily.\\n\\n- It does not break the document flow.\\n\\n- It supports responsiveness without hacks.","1. The axes in Flexbox:\\n\\n- The main axis: its direction is set by `flex-direction` (`row`, `column`).\\n\\n- The cross axis: perpendicular to the main one.\\n\\n- The properties that control them:\\n\\n- `justify-content` (the main axis).\\n\\n- `align-items` (the cross axis).\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\n2. The problems with `float`:\\n\\n- Elements \\"drop out\\" of the flow, and hacks are needed (`clearfix`).\\n\\n- Vertical alignment is difficult.\\n\\n- It is not responsive without media queries.\\n\\nConclusion:\\nFlexbox is the modern standard for most layout tasks.",".container {\\n  display: flex;\\n  flex-direction: row; /* The main axis is horizontal */\\n  justify-content: center; /* Centring along the main axis */\\n  align-items: flex-start; /* Alignment along the cross axis */\\n}"],[398,"The choice depends on the task:\\n\\n- UI libraries (Material UI, Bootstrap) — for rapid development and standard components.\\n\\n- Custom CSS — for a unique design and full control.","The advantages of UI libraries:\\n\\n- Ready-made components (buttons, forms).\\n\\n- Responsiveness out of the box.\\n\\n- A consistent design.\\n\\nThe disadvantages:\\n\\n- Limited customization.\\n\\n- Extra code if you do not use every component.\\n\\nWhen to write the CSS yourself:\\n\\n- A non-standard design.\\n\\n- The need to optimize (a minimal bundle).\\n\\n- Projects with strict performance requirements.\\n\\nExamples:\\n\\n- Libraries: Material UI, Ant Design, Bootstrap.\\n\\n- Custom CSS: Tailwind (the utility approach), Sass.\\n\\nConclusion:\\nFor an MVP or for corporate products — libraries. For unique interfaces — custom CSS.",null],[399,"Use the `addEventListener` method to subscribe to the `click` event:\\n\\n`document.querySelector(\'button\').addEventListener(\'click\', () => {\\nconsole.log(\'The button was clicked!\');\\n});`","The ways of subscribing:\\n\\n- Through `addEventListener` (the recommended way):\\n\\n- Through an HTML attribute (the obsolete method):\\n\\nThe details:\\n\\n- `addEventListener` lets you add several handlers for one event.\\n\\n- To remove a handler, use `removeEventListener`.\\n\\nConclusion:\\nAlways prefer `addEventListener`, for flexibility and better supportability of your code.","const button = document.getElementById(\'myButton\');\\nbutton.addEventListener(\'click\', handleClick);\\nfunction handleClick() {\\n  alert(\'Click!\');\\n}\\n\\n<button onclick=\\"alert(\'Click!\')\\">Click me</button>"],[400,"`opacity: 0` makes an element invisible, but it still takes up room and still responds to events. `visibility: hidden` also hides an element and keeps its place in the layout, but mouse events do not fire. `display: none` removes the element from the document flow entirely, freeing up the room it occupied.","The differences between the hiding methods:\\n\\n- `opacity: 0`:\\n\\n- The element becomes invisible, but it still takes up space in the layout.\\n\\n- It remains available for interaction (mouse events, focus and so on).\\n\\n- You can animate a smooth appearance/disappearance.\\n\\n- `visibility: hidden`:\\n\\n- The element is invisible, but it keeps its place in the layout.\\n\\n- It does not respond to mouse events (the events do not fire).\\n\\n- It can be animated with transitions.\\n\\n- `display: none`:\\n\\n- The element is removed from the document flow entirely.\\n\\n- It takes up no room and does not respond to events.\\n\\n- It cannot be animated by standard CSS means.\\n\\nA practical example:\\n\\nWhen to use which:\\n\\n- Use `opacity` for smooth transparency animations.\\n\\n- Use `visibility` when you need to hide an element but keep the layout.\\n\\n- Use `display: none` when the element needs to be removed from the document entirely.",".hidden-opacity {\\n  opacity: 0; /* Invisible, but its room is taken */\\n}\\n\\n.hidden-visibility {\\n  visibility: hidden; /* Invisible, its room is taken, but it is not clickable */\\n}\\n\\n.hidden-display {\\n  display: none; /* Removed from the layout entirely */\\n}"]]');export{e as default};